WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.984 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.984 --> 00:00:03.472 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.472 --> 00:00:06.944 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.944 --> 00:00:11.260 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.010 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: OK.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:13.600 align:middle line:90%
Let me go ahead and get started.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.400 align:middle line:84%
So today, I'm moving on to
the next unit on the syllabus

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.670 align:middle line:90%
on consumer search.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:21.880 align:middle line:84%
And if you think
back to what I was

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.660 align:middle line:84%
doing for the previous week and
then what Tobias did last week,

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.420 align:middle line:84%
it's following what is very
common in IO today, which

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.600 align:middle line:84%
is to put a tremendous emphasis
on product differentiation

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.163 align:middle line:84%
as the source of
markups in markets.

00:00:33.163 --> 00:00:36.250 align:middle line:90%


00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:41.260 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's just helpful
to think about, when we do that,

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:46.100 align:middle line:84%
we're both explaining markups
using product differentiation

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.850 align:middle line:84%
and also, whenever we're
making welfare calculations,

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:52.150 align:middle line:84%
we're attributing people's--
the product differentiation

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:55.820 align:middle line:84%
to people's underlying
true preferences for goods.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.300 align:middle line:84%
And we're assigning-- a
significant part of welfare

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.420 align:middle line:84%
is then, what are people's
epsilon ij's, and what

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.745 align:middle line:90%
is the average epsilon ij?

00:01:02.745 --> 00:01:04.870 align:middle line:84%
And that's considered part
of the utility function.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.100 align:middle line:84%
That's something we consider
when we're doing welfare.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.970 align:middle line:90%
And I guess it's interesting.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.730 align:middle line:84%
It reflects current IO, and it
also reflects historical IO.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.650 align:middle line:84%
If you look at
Tirole's IO textbook,

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.540 align:middle line:84%
Tirole does not have
a chapter on search.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.800 align:middle line:84%
Tirole exclusively does product
differentiation and repeated

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.900 align:middle line:90%
games as sources of markups.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:28.880 align:middle line:84%
But intuitively, when you
think about products--

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.820 align:middle line:84%
and a lot of important
products people buy today,

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.910 align:middle line:84%
it's unclear whether there
really is much differentiation

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.490 align:middle line:84%
or whether the differentiation
is at all sufficient to cause

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:38.090 align:middle line:90%
what--

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:39.960 align:middle line:84%
the outcomes we are
seeing in the market.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.480 align:middle line:90%
So one example is credit cards.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.760 align:middle line:84%
How many people have a
really strong preference

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.060 align:middle line:84%
for whether they have a Capital
One credit card or a Citibank

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.060 align:middle line:84%
credit card or a Bank
of America credit card?

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.700 align:middle line:84%
Is it that I really like the
color of the Citibank card

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.200 align:middle line:84%
much more than I like the color
of the Bank of America card?

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.370 align:middle line:84%
Or is it the automated
robot customer

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:02.910 align:middle line:84%
service agent I
get with Citibank?

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.670 align:middle line:84%
Is that much better than
the automated robot customer

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:05.370 align:middle line:90%
service agent--

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:08.491 align:middle line:84%
I like the voice of
this one, A versus B?

00:02:08.491 --> 00:02:09.949 align:middle line:84%
It's very hard to
think that that's

00:02:09.949 --> 00:02:11.600 align:middle line:90%
what's driving the markups.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:12.860 align:middle line:90%
Cell phone plans--

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.740 align:middle line:84%
Verizon and T-Mobile
will spend a lot of money

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.990 align:middle line:84%
having commercials with
people talking about how great

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:20.100 align:middle line:90%
their service and reception is.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.820 align:middle line:84%
But again, how many people have
very strong preference for,

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.990 align:middle line:84%
I really like the Verizon
service on my phone

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:30.920 align:middle line:84%
much more than I like the
T-Mobile service on my phone?

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.160 align:middle line:90%
Mortgages, again.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.970 align:middle line:90%
And maybe one other story.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:36.920 align:middle line:84%
If you think about even
goods where there's

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.080 align:middle line:84%
clear natural product
differentiation,

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.850 align:middle line:84%
is that product differentiation
really explaining the markup?

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:45.470 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, I
own a Hyundai Kona,

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:49.880 align:middle line:90%
which is a little, compact SUV.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:53.720 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose you-- you guys
live in Cambridge or whatever,

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.270 align:middle line:84%
or in Somerville on
top of Cambridge.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:58.280 align:middle line:84%
Suppose you want
to buy a Hyundai.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:59.070 align:middle line:90%
What can you do?

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.820 align:middle line:84%
It turns out, there
are no Hyundai dealers

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.390 align:middle line:90%
in the city of Cambridge.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:05.620 align:middle line:84%
Instead you can go up to
Arlington, you can get one.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.620 align:middle line:84%
You could go out to Lynn,
and you could get one.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.522 align:middle line:84%
You could go down to
Quincy and get one.

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:11.480 align:middle line:84%
And each of those places
is, like, half an hour

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:12.063 align:middle line:90%
away from you.

00:03:12.063 --> 00:03:14.397 align:middle line:84%
I guess Arlington is probably
the easiest from Cambridge

00:03:14.397 --> 00:03:15.350 align:middle line:90%
on public transport.

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:20.920 align:middle line:84%
And if you have a car way out
here, there's dealers in Norwood

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:24.190 align:middle line:84%
and dealers in Framingham that
you can get to on the highway.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.350 align:middle line:84%
So for me, I could get to
any of those car dealers

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:27.910 align:middle line:90%
in half an hour.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:31.030 align:middle line:84%
And so if you think about
it, the Hyundai Kona

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.730 align:middle line:84%
bought from the
Arlington Hyundai dealer

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:39.460 align:middle line:84%
versus the Hyundai Kona bought
from Boch Hyundai in Norwood,

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:42.680 align:middle line:84%
what's my preference for buying
from dealer A versus dealer B?

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:48.100 align:middle line:84%
Again, it is the exact same car
offered at both dealerships.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.600 align:middle line:84%
If you think of it
like, OK, you can

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.490 align:middle line:84%
think of this as
geographic differentiation,

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:56.260 align:middle line:84%
where, for me, I'm equally good
driving here or driving here.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.600 align:middle line:84%
But there are some people
who live right in Arlington

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.270 align:middle line:84%
for whom that is
an annoying drive.

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:06.160 align:middle line:84%
But if you think about the p
star equals c plus t model,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.710 align:middle line:84%
what is t? t is the
distance by which

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:11.350 align:middle line:84%
someone at one end of the
line prefers their product

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.260 align:middle line:84%
to the people at the
other end of the line.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:17.300 align:middle line:84%
You can do that drive
in 35, 40 minutes.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.180 align:middle line:84%
What's your
disutility of driving

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:24.010 align:middle line:84%
35 or 40 minutes one
time to go get your car

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:25.960 align:middle line:90%
or something like that?

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.380 align:middle line:90%
Is that $20?

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:28.990 align:middle line:90%
Is that $30?

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:31.610 align:middle line:84%
If you're a high-income person,
is that $50 or whatever?

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.500 align:middle line:84%
But that would then
say the prediction

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.470 align:middle line:84%
is that all these cars, the
Hyundais at all the dealers,

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:42.880 align:middle line:84%
should be marked up by $40
or $50 over the dealer cost

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:43.570 align:middle line:90%
to the--

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:45.400 align:middle line:90%
and we think that that's just--

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.350 align:middle line:84%
counterfactually,
that's just not correct.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:51.210 align:middle line:84%
Buying a car, local dealer
markups are $1,000 or something

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:51.710 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.960 align:middle line:84%
And the c plus t model is
not going to give us that.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.740 align:middle line:90%
So how do we explain that?

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:01.450 align:middle line:84%
One idea that people have is
that another important component

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:03.820 align:middle line:84%
of real-world markups
could be search costs.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:08.620 align:middle line:84%
And search costs, intuitively,
the more complicated

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:12.990 align:middle line:84%
or difficult it is to find
out what the prices are,

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.610 align:middle line:84%
the more people are
going to give up

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.480 align:middle line:84%
without finding
what the prices are,

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:19.530 align:middle line:84%
and the more that prevents the
competition that Hotelling says

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.560 align:middle line:84%
should be there for
those marginal consumers.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.950 align:middle line:84%
In the Hotelling model, if
you estimate that people--

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.960 align:middle line:90%
that there's $1,000 markup--

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:32.940 align:middle line:84%
let's suppose that you're
doing a differentiated product

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.107 align:middle line:84%
estimation and you're
imposing the supply side.

00:05:35.107 --> 00:05:37.440 align:middle line:84%
I think Tobias pointed out
that sometimes you just do it

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.130 align:middle line:84%
entirely with
demand-side moments.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:41.520 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, you also impose
the supply-side moments

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.858 align:middle line:84%
to explain what's going
on and use the cost data.

00:05:43.858 --> 00:05:45.900 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you're imposing
the supply-side moments,

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.360 align:middle line:84%
you're saying there's a $1,000
differentiation between this

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.820 align:middle line:84%
Hyundai dealer and this Hyundai
dealer, then you would get--

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.278 align:middle line:84%
it would be terrible if one of
those Hyundai dealers closed

00:05:53.278 --> 00:05:55.890 align:middle line:84%
because all those people are
getting $1,000 worth of utility

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:58.348 align:middle line:84%
from going to this one versus
that one would have that huge

00:05:58.348 --> 00:06:00.780 align:middle line:84%
loss in welfare, which we
think is probably not a correct

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.400 align:middle line:90%
answer.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.070 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I'm going
to start this with--

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.160 align:middle line:84%
Diamond has this very
nice paper from 1971,

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.560 align:middle line:84%
noting that this basic
idea that the higher is

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:13.480 align:middle line:84%
search costs, the higher
price is going to be

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.910 align:middle line:84%
seems harder to capture
than you might have guessed.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:17.060 align:middle line:90%
So here's his model.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:18.050 align:middle line:90%
You have N firms.

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:20.690 align:middle line:84%
They're producing a homogeneous
good, like the Hyundai Kona.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:21.190 align:middle line:90%
Oh, no.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:22.607 align:middle line:84%
Actually, it's not
a Hyundai Kona.

00:06:22.607 --> 00:06:27.100 align:middle line:84%
So this is going
to be credit cards.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:31.030 align:middle line:84%
So N firms produce a
homogeneous good at cost c.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:35.650 align:middle line:84%
So that cost c is it
costs them 8.99 or 9%

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:37.540 align:middle line:84%
to loan you money
on a credit card.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:40.060 align:middle line:90%
And so c is 9%.

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:42.130 align:middle line:84%
They're a continuum
of consumers,

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:45.340 align:middle line:84%
and they have identical
multiunit demands D of p.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:48.100 align:middle line:84%
So if they give me
a credit card at 9%,

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:50.150 align:middle line:90%
I may borrow $10,000 on it.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:53.840 align:middle line:84%
If they give me a credit card at
18%, I may borrow $3,000 on it.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:56.230 align:middle line:84%
But there's some
downward-sloping demand function

00:06:56.230 --> 00:06:57.813 align:middle line:84%
for how many units
you're going to buy

00:06:57.813 --> 00:07:00.880 align:middle line:84%
from the firm as a
function of the price.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:03.167 align:middle line:84%
And I'm doing the credit
cards rather than cars

00:07:03.167 --> 00:07:05.500 align:middle line:84%
because it's important that
you're buying more than one,

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:09.040 align:middle line:84%
whereas cars, typically,
you buy exactly one.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:12.190 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to assume that
p minus c times D of p

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:13.760 align:middle line:90%
is a concave function.

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.660 align:middle line:84%
It's got some finite
monopoly price pm.

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:19.160 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to consider
the following game.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:22.990 align:middle line:84%
First, the firms simultaneously
choose their prices.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:27.490 align:middle line:84%
And then customers have
to pay s every time

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:28.785 align:middle line:90%
they get a price quote.

00:07:28.785 --> 00:07:30.160 align:middle line:84%
And we're going
to want to assume

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.270 align:middle line:84%
that they search optimally, get
some number of price quotes.

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:36.460 align:middle line:84%
And then after getting some
number of price quotes,

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:39.310 align:middle line:84%
they buy D of p units from
the lowest-priced firm

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:41.230 align:middle line:90%
that they found.

00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:45.730 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to want to
have some optimality in this--

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:47.740 align:middle line:84%
search is somehow
optimal, given how much

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:49.575 align:middle line:84%
the cost of every
search is going to be.

00:07:49.575 --> 00:07:52.540 align:middle line:90%


00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:55.840 align:middle line:84%
One immediate subtlety is we
need some new solution concept

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:57.100 align:middle line:90%
other than Nash equilibrium.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.830 align:middle line:84%
Because in a Nash
equilibrium, everyone

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:04.210 align:middle line:84%
behaves as if they know the
strategies of the other players.

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:08.260 align:middle line:84%
And so if you think about this
as an N plus 1 player game,

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:12.220 align:middle line:84%
there's N firms and
one consumer buying,

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:14.350 align:middle line:84%
then in Nash equilibrium,
you're implicitly

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:16.700 align:middle line:84%
assuming that the consumer
knows the firm's strategies.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:18.700 align:middle line:84%
And if the consumer knows
the firm's strategies,

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.200 align:middle line:84%
their strategies
are their prices.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.140 align:middle line:84%
And the consumer can just buy
from the lowest-priced firm

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:27.430 align:middle line:84%
and not have to pay the
s to find that firm.

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:30.610 align:middle line:90%
So what do we do?

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:34.900 align:middle line:84%
Diamond, essentially,
in his paper,

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:38.299 align:middle line:84%
made this an N
plus 2 player game.

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:44.020 align:middle line:84%
So you have the N firms,
you have the one consumer,

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:49.240 align:middle line:84%
and then you have the one
player I'll call "nature."

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:52.510 align:middle line:90%
And what the firms do is--

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:55.090 align:middle line:84%
so first, stage
one is the firm's--

00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:58.120 align:middle line:90%
they choose p1 up to pN.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:00.490 align:middle line:90%
So that is the first thing--

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:03.910 align:middle line:90%
actually, I need a timeline.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:10.780 align:middle line:84%
So p equals 1, the firm's
move, and they choose p1 up

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:12.880 align:middle line:90%
through pN.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.680 align:middle line:84%
And then at stage
two, nature moves.

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.096 align:middle line:84%
And what nature does is
chooses a random permutation.

00:09:21.096 --> 00:09:25.380 align:middle line:90%


00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.790 align:middle line:84%
So it's if nature builds
a room, and this room

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:34.920 align:middle line:90%
has doors numbered 1 through N.

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:37.440 align:middle line:84%
And so the firms
pick their prices,

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.720 align:middle line:84%
and then nature takes firm 1 and
sticks it behind a closed door.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.610 align:middle line:90%
Say it randomly picks door 7.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:47.880 align:middle line:84%
And then it takes firm 2 and
puts it behind another door,

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.060 align:middle line:90%
say, door 1.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:51.630 align:middle line:84%
And so nature does this
random permutation.

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:58.640 align:middle line:84%
And then t equals 3, the
consumer starts opening doors.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:02.610 align:middle line:90%


00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:07.530 align:middle line:84%
And each door you open,
there's a cost of s.

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:13.260 align:middle line:84%
So in equilibrium, I'm going to
know what p1, p2, and pN are.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:16.080 align:middle line:84%
But I don't know which
firm is behind which door.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:20.620 align:middle line:84%
So then I open door
number 1, see who's there.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:23.825 align:middle line:84%
If I don't like that price, it
seems high, I may open door 2.

00:10:23.825 --> 00:10:25.200 align:middle line:84%
If it seems like
it's still worth

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:27.408 align:middle line:84%
paying s to open another
door, I'd open another door,

00:10:27.408 --> 00:10:29.370 align:middle line:90%
and I continue like that.

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:31.920 align:middle line:84%
And so then Diamond
asked, what's

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.470 align:middle line:90%
the equilibrium of that model?

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:38.190 align:middle line:84%
And the thought was that you
might get an answer that says,

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:39.960 align:middle line:84%
when search costs
are higher, prices

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:41.520 align:middle line:90%
are higher than they would--

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.550 align:middle line:84%
without the random permutation,
this is a Bertrand competition

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:45.168 align:middle line:90%
model.

00:10:45.168 --> 00:10:46.710 align:middle line:84%
And so you might
think that you would

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:50.310 align:middle line:84%
get a just Bertrand outcome,
everyone's p star equal c.

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:53.130 align:middle line:84%
But then as search costs
go up, maybe prices

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:54.550 align:middle line:90%
go up in equilibrium.

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:58.407 align:middle line:84%
And they do, but they do
more than Diamond wanted.

00:10:58.407 --> 00:11:00.990 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, he started this
literature with this thing that

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:03.830 align:middle line:84%
sometimes people call
the "Diamond paradox."

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:07.780 align:middle line:84%
If you take any search
cost s that's strictly

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:09.790 align:middle line:84%
positive and less than
the consumer surplus,

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:15.910 align:middle line:84%
the monopoly price, then any
perfect Bayesian equilibrium

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:18.910 align:middle line:84%
in which any consumers
search has all firms

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:21.250 align:middle line:90%
pricing at the monopoly price.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:24.250 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, the only
equilibria of this model

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:27.020 align:middle line:84%
are it's a discontinuous
jump in prices.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:29.020 align:middle line:84%
As soon as there's
any search cost, even

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:31.600 align:middle line:84%
a penny, the only equilibrium
is everyone charges

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:34.450 align:middle line:84%
a monopoly price instead
of everyone charges c.

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:38.560 align:middle line:84%
So it says, Bertrand competition
is an extraordinarily fragile

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:42.170 align:middle line:84%
result. Bertrand competition
relied on zero search costs.

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:45.160 align:middle line:84%
You put any search cost in, you
don't just get a little markup,

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:47.080 align:middle line:90%
you get the monopoly markup.

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:49.210 align:middle line:84%
Now, we don't
believe this, but I

00:11:49.210 --> 00:11:52.970 align:middle line:84%
think it's good to
see why this happens.

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:56.800 align:middle line:84%
So first, the argument
that this is an equilibrium

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:58.180 align:middle line:90%
is straightforward.

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:02.260 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose you have a world
where all the firms charge pm,

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:04.060 align:middle line:84%
and you know that
everyone charges pm.

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:04.760 align:middle line:90%
You've grown up.

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:06.302 align:middle line:84%
You've lived your
life in this world.

00:12:06.302 --> 00:12:09.100 align:middle line:84%
You know that, yes, everyone
charges a monopoly price.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:11.255 align:middle line:84%
You open the first--
you get assigned--

00:12:11.255 --> 00:12:12.380 align:middle line:90%
you have to play this game.

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:14.320 align:middle line:84%
You open a door,
and you're like, oh,

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:15.805 align:middle line:84%
what does cell
phone service cost?

00:12:15.805 --> 00:12:19.270 align:middle line:84%
Oh, it's $59.99 a month, even
though it has marginal cost

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:20.840 align:middle line:90%
of zero for them to provide it.

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:21.740 align:middle line:90%
What do you do?

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:23.270 align:middle line:84%
Do you keep opening
the other doors?

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:25.120 align:middle line:84%
You're like, no, every
cell phone company

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:27.280 align:middle line:90%
charges $59.99 a month.

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:29.800 align:middle line:90%
Why keep opening doors?

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:32.380 align:middle line:84%
I open the first door, I see
the monopoly price, I pay it,

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:34.660 align:middle line:90%
and then I'm done.

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:40.250 align:middle line:84%
And could a firm cut its
price and gain consumers?

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:43.510 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is no, because
when consumers expect everyone

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:45.460 align:middle line:84%
to be charging the
monopoly price,

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:49.400 align:middle line:84%
everyone stops at the first door
and just buys from that firm.

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:51.730 align:middle line:84%
And so suppose you
have a lower price--

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:55.840 align:middle line:84%
well, then consumers-- and
you get assigned to room 7.

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.780 align:middle line:84%
If a consumer comes to you
first and opens your door,

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:02.590 align:middle line:84%
they'll be like, oh, bonus,
I get to pay $35 a month

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:04.580 align:middle line:90%
instead of $59.99 a month.

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:07.942 align:middle line:84%
But it's only the consumers
who open that door first

00:13:07.942 --> 00:13:08.650 align:middle line:90%
who buy from you.

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:09.660 align:middle line:84%
And they would have
bought from if you'd

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.340 align:middle line:90%
charged the monopoly price.

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:14.190 align:middle line:84%
So the firms don't get
any extra consumers

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:18.060 align:middle line:90%
by charging a lower price.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:20.860 align:middle line:84%
If everyone charged a monopoly
price, consumers don't search.

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:23.380 align:middle line:84%
If no one searches, all
firms charge monopoly price.

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:24.460 align:middle line:90%
That's an equilibrium.

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:27.180 align:middle line:90%


00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:32.070 align:middle line:84%
The less immediately obvious
part of this argument

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:35.820 align:middle line:84%
is that there's no other
pure strategy equilibrium.

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:38.760 align:middle line:84%
To think about
that, just imagine

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:44.070 align:middle line:84%
we had some other
equilibrium and the firms

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.000 align:middle line:90%
charged different prices.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:49.270 align:middle line:84%
So maybe this is
the monopoly price.

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:51.360 align:middle line:84%
And then some firms
charge a monopoly price,

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.630 align:middle line:84%
but some firms charge less
than the monopoly price.

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:56.003 align:middle line:90%


00:13:56.003 --> 00:13:57.420 align:middle line:84%
Well, the argument
is going to be,

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:59.370 align:middle line:84%
you can't have an
equilibrium like this

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:02.730 align:middle line:84%
where somebody charges a lower
price than everybody else.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:07.680 align:middle line:84%
Because if you're charging a
lower price than everybody else,

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.400 align:middle line:84%
I could raise my
price by epsilon.

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:14.335 align:middle line:84%
Again, anyone who opens
the door and sees my--

00:14:14.335 --> 00:14:16.710 align:middle line:84%
so consumers were expecting
that there was some firm that

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:19.140 align:middle line:90%
charged $35 a month.

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:23.430 align:middle line:90%
And I go to $35.50 a month.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:25.090 align:middle line:90%
So consumers open that door.

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:28.040 align:middle line:84%
They may think, oh, no one
was supposed to charge $35.50.

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:30.660 align:middle line:84%
There was supposed to be a
firm at $35, a firm at $36,

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:32.580 align:middle line:90%
and a bunch of firms at $40.

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:34.730 align:middle line:90%
But I just found $35.50.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:35.950 align:middle line:90%
What do I do?

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:39.390 align:middle line:84%
Well, the most you could expect
to save by opening another door

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:40.420 align:middle line:90%
is $0.50.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:43.130 align:middle line:84%
Because you thought that
there was someone at $35.

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:44.610 align:middle line:90%
You didn't find the guy at $35.

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:48.120 align:middle line:84%
But that's the best you're
going to think to do.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:51.710 align:middle line:84%
As long as I keep the amount, I
raise the price less than the s,

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:55.780 align:middle line:84%
anyone who opens my door first
is still going to buy from me.

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:57.580 align:middle line:90%
So I don't lose any--

00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:59.840 align:middle line:84%
anyone who ever opens my
door is going to buy from me.

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:03.737 align:middle line:84%
So therefore, I can't lose any
consumers by this price range.

00:15:03.737 --> 00:15:06.070 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, I'm always going
to raise my price by epsilon

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:08.160 align:middle line:90%
over what people expect.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.500 align:middle line:84%
And so that makes it
impossible to have

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:13.480 align:middle line:84%
an equilibrium where somebody
is below everybody else.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:15.300 align:middle line:84%
It also even makes
it impossible to have

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:17.430 align:middle line:84%
an argument where you have
two people tied for the lowest

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:17.930 align:middle line:90%
price.

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:21.040 align:middle line:84%
Because again, I just
make an epsilon increase.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:23.257 align:middle line:84%
Anyone who opens
my door, as long

00:15:23.257 --> 00:15:25.590 align:middle line:84%
as I'm within epsilon of the
lowest price in the market,

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:26.965 align:middle line:84%
they're all going
to buy from me.

00:15:26.965 --> 00:15:30.233 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to have to be
that you can't have anyone--

00:15:30.233 --> 00:15:31.650 align:middle line:84%
the lowest-priced
firms are always

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:33.540 align:middle line:84%
going to want to raise their
price by epsilon if they're

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:35.150 align:middle line:90%
less than the monopoly price.

00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:38.220 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, I can't have
anyone charging greater

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:39.330 align:middle line:90%
than the monopoly price.

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:42.600 align:middle line:84%
Because if they're charging
greater than the monopoly price

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:46.343 align:middle line:84%
and not selling anything,
they make no money.

00:15:46.343 --> 00:15:47.760 align:middle line:84%
If they are selling
things, they'd

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:51.390 align:middle line:84%
be better off going down to
the monopoly price itself.

00:15:51.390 --> 00:15:53.040 align:middle line:84%
So the only equilibrium
of this model

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:55.235 align:middle line:84%
is everyone charges
monopoly prices.

00:15:55.235 --> 00:15:58.020 align:middle line:90%


00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:03.080 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's a useful
thought experiment,

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:05.985 align:middle line:84%
but it's obviously not what
we want to do in practice.

00:16:05.985 --> 00:16:07.610 align:middle line:84%
And so then where
the search literature

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:10.040 align:middle line:84%
has gone is thinking
about, what happens--

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:12.380 align:middle line:84%
what can we do with
search to get search

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:15.710 align:middle line:84%
to have a continuous effect
like we think it probably

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:16.975 align:middle line:90%
does-- should have?

00:16:16.975 --> 00:16:18.475 align:middle line:84%
And what's wrong
with this argument?

00:16:18.475 --> 00:16:23.330 align:middle line:90%


00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:26.660 align:middle line:84%
One way in which this
literature has gone,

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:28.490 align:middle line:84%
and which is
completely realistic,

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:32.390 align:middle line:84%
is models with heterogeneity
in search costs, where there

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:34.100 align:middle line:84%
are some consumers
who have higher search

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:36.850 align:middle line:84%
costs and some consumers
who have lower search costs.

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:40.250 align:middle line:90%


00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:41.810 align:middle line:90%
And it turns out that--

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:44.090 align:middle line:84%
it's a nice literature
because it actually

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:46.922 align:middle line:84%
turns out it's going to explain
two phenomena simultaneously.

00:16:46.922 --> 00:16:48.380 align:middle line:84%
One is it's going
to give us models

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:51.050 align:middle line:84%
that do have positive
markups that are

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:52.980 align:middle line:90%
continuous in the search cost.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:54.680 align:middle line:84%
And then the other
thing it gets us

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:58.400 align:middle line:84%
is price dispersion, where
different firms charge

00:16:58.400 --> 00:16:59.490 align:middle line:90%
different prices.

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:02.420 align:middle line:84%
And I think in the
car dealer example,

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:04.670 align:middle line:84%
you do expect that every
different car dealer has

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:07.609 align:middle line:84%
a different price that they're
willing to settle for on the car

00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:10.526 align:middle line:84%
if you negotiate long enough
and that every different firm

00:17:10.526 --> 00:17:12.234 align:middle line:84%
is going to charge
you a different price.

00:17:12.234 --> 00:17:15.079 align:middle line:90%


00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:16.859 align:middle line:90%
So how would this model go?

00:17:16.859 --> 00:17:20.369 align:middle line:84%
So again, let's stick with
my credit card example.

00:17:20.369 --> 00:17:21.776 align:middle line:90%
So I have N firms.

00:17:21.776 --> 00:17:23.359 align:middle line:84%
They're all producing
that homogeneous

00:17:23.359 --> 00:17:27.140 align:middle line:84%
good at the same common,
constant marginal cost.

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:29.510 align:middle line:84%
Again, I'm going to
let them choose prices.

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:32.130 align:middle line:84%
Again, I'm going to have the
same continuum of consumers.

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:35.690 align:middle line:84%
Each consumer has a
multiunit demand D of p.

00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:38.270 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to have
consumers searching optimally,

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:40.760 align:middle line:90%
as in the Diamond model.

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:43.910 align:middle line:84%
But instead of the Diamond model
where everyone had search cost

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:47.870 align:middle line:84%
s, I'm going to have
multiple types of consumers.

00:17:47.870 --> 00:17:50.490 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to have a fraction
mu of the consumers.

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:53.326 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes people refer to
these guys as "shoppers."

00:17:53.326 --> 00:17:56.730 align:middle line:90%


00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:59.440 align:middle line:84%
Shoppers have a
negative search cost.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:02.790 align:middle line:84%
So shoppers actually get
utility out of comparing prices

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:04.830 align:middle line:90%
at different firms.

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:07.080 align:middle line:84%
And you may think
that's unrealistic.

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:10.170 align:middle line:84%
But I mean, there
are some people

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:11.820 align:middle line:90%
who like shopping as a hobby.

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:13.320 align:middle line:84%
There are some
graduate students who

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:16.263 align:middle line:84%
love to tell me that, I booked
this crazy plane ticket where

00:18:16.263 --> 00:18:18.180 align:middle line:84%
I fly to here, and then
I have a return ticket

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:19.840 align:middle line:90%
that's booked out of this city.

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:22.690 align:middle line:84%
But then I'm going to get off in
this city and save that thing.

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:24.940 align:middle line:90%
And I'm saving $7 by doing this.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.630 align:middle line:84%
And you have to think,
did you spend, like,

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:30.630 align:middle line:84%
14.5 hours figuring out
some really clever way

00:18:30.630 --> 00:18:32.190 align:middle line:90%
you could save $7?

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:35.038 align:middle line:84%
It must be that you got
a great deal of utility

00:18:35.038 --> 00:18:37.080 align:middle line:84%
out of figuring out all
of the different ways one

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:39.780 align:middle line:84%
could fly from A to B and
feeling that you were--

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:40.330 align:middle line:90%
whatever.

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:40.872 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:18:40.872 --> 00:18:44.280 align:middle line:84%
But there are some people
who just will enjoy shopping

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:46.570 align:middle line:84%
and will do more shopping
than seems reasonable.

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:50.490 align:middle line:84%
And so I'll model them by having
this negative search cost.

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:52.740 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to have
a fraction, 1 minus mu

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:55.500 align:middle line:84%
of consumers, who have the more
traditional positive search

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:57.010 align:middle line:90%
costs.

00:18:57.010 --> 00:18:59.040 align:middle line:84%
And at least at
first, I'm just going

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:03.510 align:middle line:84%
to assume that these people have
search costs in some interval s

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:04.890 align:middle line:90%
lower bar, s upper bar.

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:08.440 align:middle line:84%
It could be distributed
with some density.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:11.520 align:middle line:84%
But they are all less than the
consumer surplus, the monopoly

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:12.318 align:middle line:90%
price.

00:19:12.318 --> 00:19:14.610 align:middle line:84%
The reason for putting this
upper bound on search costs

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:17.220 align:middle line:84%
is, if your search costs
are less than the consumer

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:18.868 align:middle line:84%
surplus of the monopoly
price, then you

00:19:18.868 --> 00:19:20.910 align:middle line:84%
run into this problem of
just those people aren't

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:22.117 align:middle line:90%
going to search at all.

00:19:22.117 --> 00:19:23.950 align:middle line:84%
And then they're not
going to search at all,

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:25.510 align:middle line:90%
so then no market exists.

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:27.210 align:middle line:84%
So I want search
costs low enough

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:31.380 align:middle line:84%
so that consumers are going
to want to search and buy,

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:32.770 align:middle line:90%
even if the prices are high.

00:19:32.770 --> 00:19:35.353 align:middle line:84%
And I don't have to worry about
that edge case of what happens

00:19:35.353 --> 00:19:36.820 align:middle line:90%
if prices are high-- too high.

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:39.160 align:middle line:84%
So this distribution
of search costs.

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:44.190 align:middle line:84%
So I've just got some interval
s lower bar, s upper bar, where

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:45.510 align:middle line:90%
I have a density of consumers.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:48.450 align:middle line:84%
And then you can think of it
as a point mass of consumers

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:50.460 align:middle line:90%
with negative search costs.

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:56.220 align:middle line:84%
And it's just a
simplification, but it's

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:58.720 align:middle line:84%
a simplification that
implies that these consumers,

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:00.350 align:middle line:84%
it's easy to solve
for what they do.

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:02.192 align:middle line:84%
What they do is they
visit every single firm

00:20:02.192 --> 00:20:04.150 align:middle line:84%
and then buy from the
lowest-priced firm that's

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:06.680 align:middle line:90%
out there.

00:20:06.680 --> 00:20:10.760 align:middle line:84%
So what's Stahl's
result on this model?

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:13.430 align:middle line:84%
Part a of the
theorem, this model

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:16.760 align:middle line:84%
has no pure strategy
equilibrium,

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:20.150 align:middle line:84%
but it does have at least
one symmetric mixed strategy

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:21.320 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:24.380 align:middle line:84%
And in this symmetric
mixed strategy equilibrium,

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:28.340 align:middle line:84%
the firms choose prices from
an atomless distribution

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:31.310 align:middle line:84%
F that has some support
p lower bar, p upper bar.

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:34.670 align:middle line:84%
And that support does not
contain marginal cost.

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:36.830 align:middle line:84%
So what's going to
typically happen here

00:20:36.830 --> 00:20:38.250 align:middle line:90%
is, if you look at pricing--

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:42.416 align:middle line:90%


00:20:42.416 --> 00:20:43.700 align:middle line:90%
did I give it a name?

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:45.500 align:middle line:90%
I did not give it a name.

00:20:45.500 --> 00:20:49.130 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to have this
distribution where there's--

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:52.610 align:middle line:84%
you could price it marginal cost
in Bertrand, but firms don't.

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:55.730 align:middle line:84%
Instead, there's some
level p lower bar,

00:20:55.730 --> 00:20:58.670 align:middle line:84%
and there's some
level p upper bar.

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:03.140 align:middle line:84%
And then what firms do
is there's some density.

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:04.680 align:middle line:90%
It might look like this.

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:07.350 align:middle line:84%
And so what firms do
is they choose prices--

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:09.090 align:middle line:84%
use a mixed strategy,
choose prices

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:11.260 align:middle line:84%
from a density that
looks like this.

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:14.320 align:middle line:84%
And so prices, from the consumer
perspective, are random.

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:17.760 align:middle line:84%
And then, in some sense,
this mixed strategy

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:19.980 align:middle line:84%
on the firm's part then
rationalizes the search

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:21.010 align:middle line:90%
on the consumer part.

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:24.450 align:middle line:84%
Because if the firms
are mixing over prices,

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:25.960 align:middle line:90%
you go and find a firm's price.

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:28.252 align:middle line:84%
What you're getting is one
draw from the mixed strategy

00:21:28.252 --> 00:21:28.830 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:29.850 align:middle line:90%
You may get a high draw.

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:31.245 align:middle line:90%
You may get a low draw.

00:21:31.245 --> 00:21:33.370 align:middle line:84%
You may get a high draw
and decide to search again.

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:38.880 align:middle line:84%
You may get a low draw and
decide to stop your search.

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:40.920 align:middle line:90%
Clearly, if you draw--

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:43.895 align:middle line:84%
if the first firm you draw
actually has price p lower bar,

00:21:43.895 --> 00:21:45.270 align:middle line:84%
you're always
going to stop there

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:48.390 align:middle line:84%
because you know that's
the best you can do.

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:52.680 align:middle line:84%
If the firm has price p upper
bar, if your search cost is

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:55.500 align:middle line:84%
really high, like if your
search cost is near s upper bar,

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:57.210 align:middle line:90%
you're going to buy--

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:58.980 align:middle line:84%
the model is going
to be such that you

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:01.900 align:middle line:84%
are going to buy from
that price if you find it.

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:04.020 align:middle line:84%
But then someone
with a lower s may

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.510 align:middle line:84%
decide not to buy there
and only buy if they

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:07.715 align:middle line:90%
find this price or better.

00:22:07.715 --> 00:22:15.330 align:middle line:90%


00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:16.465 align:middle line:90%
So, Remarks.

00:22:16.465 --> 00:22:18.090 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, the mixed
equilibrium can be seen

00:22:18.090 --> 00:22:19.215 align:middle line:90%
as a potential explanation.

00:22:19.215 --> 00:22:20.220 align:middle line:90%
Yes, question.

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:21.060 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:25.210 align:middle line:84%
So intuitively, why do you need
the shoppers in this model?

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:28.470 align:middle line:84%
Because at first glance,
it would seem like just

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:31.770 align:middle line:84%
by having the dispersion
in search costs

00:22:31.770 --> 00:22:34.027 align:middle line:84%
would generate a
dispersion in prices.

00:22:34.027 --> 00:22:34.860 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: Yeah.

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:37.620 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, you
get this same--

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:40.120 align:middle line:84%
it's the same Diamond
argument would work here.

00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:42.750 align:middle line:84%
So let's suppose
that we had these--

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:44.400 align:middle line:90%
I took this away.

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:48.000 align:middle line:84%
And then imagine I tried to
have an equilibrium like this.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:50.310 align:middle line:84%
And then think about
the firm that's

00:22:50.310 --> 00:22:51.915 align:middle line:90%
charging price p lower bar.

00:22:51.915 --> 00:22:54.560 align:middle line:90%


00:22:54.560 --> 00:22:56.310 align:middle line:84%
So to have a mixed
strategy equilibrium,

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:59.010 align:middle line:84%
I have to be indifferent
over all these prices.

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:01.800 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose I deviate, and
instead of charging p lower bar,

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:05.580 align:middle line:84%
I charge p lower
bar plus epsilon.

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:07.030 align:middle line:90%
Again, the same argument.

00:23:07.030 --> 00:23:09.630 align:middle line:84%
Any time anyone
gets a quote from me

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:14.580 align:middle line:84%
and they find price p lower
bar plus epsilon, as long

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:21.480 align:middle line:84%
as consumer surplus at p
lower bar plus epsilon,

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:25.440 align:middle line:84%
consumer surplus at p lower
bar minus consumer surplus

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:29.710 align:middle line:84%
at that is less than s, they're
going to have to buy from me.

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:34.750 align:middle line:84%
Because the most they could gain
by finding p lower bar is this.

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:37.390 align:middle line:84%
If this is less than
s, they would stop.

00:23:37.390 --> 00:23:40.150 align:middle line:84%
So you still get this epsilon
over a cutting argument

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:41.843 align:middle line:84%
that says, this can't
be an equilibrium,

00:23:41.843 --> 00:23:44.260 align:middle line:84%
because if everyone knows that
everyone charges at least p

00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:46.843 align:middle line:84%
lower bar, then everyone would
want to charge at least p lower

00:23:46.843 --> 00:23:47.930 align:middle line:90%
bar plus epsilon.

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:49.750 align:middle line:84%
And if everyone knows everyone
wants to charge at least p lower

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:51.292 align:middle line:84%
bar plus epsilon,
then everyone wants

00:23:51.292 --> 00:23:53.860 align:middle line:84%
to charge at least p lower bar
plus 2 epsilon and the thing

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:54.210 align:middle line:90%
collapses.

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:56.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you're going
to want someone who's always

00:23:56.350 --> 00:23:57.150 align:middle line:90%
willing to keep searching.

00:23:57.150 --> 00:23:57.820 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: So
you want someone

00:23:57.820 --> 00:24:00.730 align:middle line:84%
who is always willing to keep
searching, that you may lose--

00:24:00.730 --> 00:24:02.920 align:middle line:84%
you need to explain, how
could anyone ever charge

00:24:02.920 --> 00:24:04.280 align:middle line:90%
the lowest possible price?

00:24:04.280 --> 00:24:07.570 align:middle line:84%
And there, it could be, if you
raise your price by epsilon,

00:24:07.570 --> 00:24:09.460 align:middle line:84%
there are those
shoppers who you're

00:24:09.460 --> 00:24:12.642 align:middle line:90%
going to lose by doing that.

00:24:12.642 --> 00:24:14.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in this
model, the consumers

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:15.970 align:middle line:90%
always know the distribution?

00:24:15.970 --> 00:24:17.553 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: So
in this model, we're

00:24:17.553 --> 00:24:19.660 align:middle line:84%
assuming the consumers
know the distribution.

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:21.760 align:middle line:90%
That is, this is where--

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:23.748 align:middle line:84%
obviously, you can have
models where consumers

00:24:23.748 --> 00:24:25.040 align:middle line:90%
have to learn the distribution.

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:26.950 align:middle line:84%
But here, we're sticking
with the equilibrium

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:29.358 align:middle line:84%
notion of consumers are best
responding to the firm's

00:24:29.358 --> 00:24:31.900 align:middle line:84%
strategies, and the firms are
best responding to the consumer

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:33.200 align:middle line:90%
strategies.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:33.760 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:37.165 align:middle line:84%
And I think that one doesn't
strike me as so unreasonable

00:24:37.165 --> 00:24:38.890 align:middle line:84%
that, like, on the
credit card market,

00:24:38.890 --> 00:24:40.307 align:middle line:84%
I know there are
credit cards that

00:24:40.307 --> 00:24:42.890 align:middle line:84%
offer a good deal and credit
cards that offer a bad deal.

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:45.760 align:middle line:84%
And I go out there and I start
clicking on credit cards,

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:47.260 align:middle line:84%
and I look at this
credit card and I

00:24:47.260 --> 00:24:48.552 align:middle line:90%
see what rate they're offering.

00:24:48.552 --> 00:24:51.230 align:middle line:84%
And certainly, the credit card
offers you get in the mail,

00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:53.740 align:middle line:84%
they all have different
rates on them.

00:24:53.740 --> 00:24:55.750 align:middle line:84%
I kind of know what
the distribution is,

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:57.250 align:middle line:84%
and I know what's
going to take me--

00:24:57.250 --> 00:25:00.290 align:middle line:84%
I get some sense of how good
I'm doing by following it.

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:06.260 align:middle line:84%
And like we said, they know
the equilibrium strategies.

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:08.740 align:middle line:84%
They don't know the
actual distribution.

00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:12.250 align:middle line:84%
So if a firm deviates, it
changes the distribution

00:25:12.250 --> 00:25:14.120 align:middle line:90%
and consumers don't know that.

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:17.810 align:middle line:84%
So consumers know what's the
normal distribution of prices.

00:25:17.810 --> 00:25:18.310 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:21.250 align:middle line:90%


00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:23.032 align:middle line:90%
So it's a mixed equilibrium.

00:25:23.032 --> 00:25:24.490 align:middle line:84%
You can think of
this as explaining

00:25:24.490 --> 00:25:28.090 align:middle line:90%
why prices differ across firms.

00:25:28.090 --> 00:25:32.470 align:middle line:84%
Prices in this model move
continuously with search costs,

00:25:32.470 --> 00:25:34.420 align:middle line:84%
and they move continuously
with the fraction

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:36.680 align:middle line:84%
of consumers who are shoppers
in the way you'd expect.

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:41.800 align:middle line:84%
So either as the fraction
of shoppers goes to 1 or as

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:45.890 align:middle line:84%
s upper bar goes to 0, prices
collapse to Bertrand pricing.

00:25:45.890 --> 00:25:53.110 align:middle line:84%
So if this is what it looks like
with what I've drawn, if I make

00:25:53.110 --> 00:25:56.887 align:middle line:84%
s upper bar down here, so
if I make all the consumer

00:25:56.887 --> 00:25:58.720 align:middle line:84%
search costs tiny, what
you're going to find

00:25:58.720 --> 00:26:02.140 align:middle line:84%
is that, if this is c, the
distribution looks like that.

00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:03.910 align:middle line:84%
That is, the
distribution collapses

00:26:03.910 --> 00:26:08.030 align:middle line:84%
towards a point mass on p
equals c as the s goes to 0.

00:26:08.030 --> 00:26:11.140 align:middle line:84%
It also collapses
towards a point mass on c

00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:13.800 align:middle line:84%
as the fraction of
shoppers goes to 1.

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:18.660 align:middle line:90%


00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:20.520 align:middle line:84%
These equilibria of
this kind of model

00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:23.970 align:middle line:84%
often have U-shaped
distributions when N is large.

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:26.790 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, if
you're a firm and you

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:29.820 align:middle line:84%
know you're competing for a
large number of consumers,

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:32.730 align:middle line:90%
you have two good strategies.

00:26:32.730 --> 00:26:37.890 align:middle line:90%


00:26:37.890 --> 00:26:44.700 align:middle line:84%
One strategy is typically
priced near p lower bar

00:26:44.700 --> 00:26:46.563 align:middle line:84%
and priced near p
lower bar in hope

00:26:46.563 --> 00:26:48.480 align:middle line:84%
to have the single lowest
price and to get all

00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:50.550 align:middle line:90%
the shoppers to buy from you.

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:52.920 align:middle line:84%
And then the other
strategy is price up here

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:56.160 align:middle line:84%
near the monopoly price
and figure that, I'm never

00:26:56.160 --> 00:26:57.430 align:middle line:90%
going to win all the shoppers.

00:26:57.430 --> 00:26:59.888 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to get the people
who come to my store first

00:26:59.888 --> 00:27:01.680 align:middle line:90%
and have a high s.

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:03.473 align:middle line:84%
And so often, you
get-- there's often

00:27:03.473 --> 00:27:04.890 align:middle line:84%
less point of being
in the middle,

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:07.015 align:middle line:84%
because in the
middle, the shoppers--

00:27:07.015 --> 00:27:08.640 align:middle line:84%
you're never going
to get the shoppers.

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:10.320 align:middle line:84%
And the nonshoppers, you
would have gotten anyway

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:11.230 align:middle line:90%
with a higher price.

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:14.590 align:middle line:84%
So you often see these
distributions that sort of look

00:27:14.590 --> 00:27:15.140 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:15.807 align:middle line:90%
They're bimodal.

00:27:15.807 --> 00:27:20.950 align:middle line:90%


00:27:20.950 --> 00:27:23.290 align:middle line:84%
The limit as N
goes to infinity is

00:27:23.290 --> 00:27:25.630 align:middle line:84%
perhaps not what
you might expect,

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:26.920 align:middle line:90%
but it is interesting itself.

00:27:26.920 --> 00:27:29.560 align:middle line:84%
As N goes to infinity, with the
way this distribution changes,

00:27:29.560 --> 00:27:31.660 align:middle line:90%
is it goes like this.

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:35.710 align:middle line:84%
You have a small number
who go for the nonshoppers.

00:27:35.710 --> 00:27:37.420 align:middle line:84%
And then you have
a huge mass of them

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:39.410 align:middle line:90%
up here at the monopoly price.

00:27:39.410 --> 00:27:44.500 align:middle line:84%
So this says, in
some sense, Tobias

00:27:44.500 --> 00:27:47.450 align:middle line:84%
talked about cereal
product proliferation

00:27:47.450 --> 00:27:49.742 align:middle line:84%
and there being a million
cereals or whatever.

00:27:49.742 --> 00:27:52.450 align:middle line:84%
This says, if you have a million
credit card companies out there,

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:54.228 align:middle line:84%
maybe what you would
see is almost all

00:27:54.228 --> 00:27:56.770 align:middle line:84%
of them are ripping people off
and just charging the monopoly

00:27:56.770 --> 00:27:57.530 align:middle line:90%
price.

00:27:57.530 --> 00:27:59.488 align:middle line:84%
And there are a few of
them out there competing

00:27:59.488 --> 00:28:01.220 align:middle line:90%
for the sophisticated consumers.

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:03.670 align:middle line:84%
And we could see this bimodal
thing, where most people are

00:28:03.670 --> 00:28:04.587 align:middle line:90%
at the monopoly price.

00:28:04.587 --> 00:28:07.690 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, this model does
say, as N goes to infinity--

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:12.550 align:middle line:84%
as N goes to infinity, they
almost all end up up here.

00:28:12.550 --> 00:28:15.250 align:middle line:84%
One argument for that is,
if you think about it,

00:28:15.250 --> 00:28:17.800 align:middle line:84%
let's suppose that you had
even a constant fraction

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:18.460 align:middle line:90%
of the firms.

00:28:18.460 --> 00:28:23.020 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that alpha N
firms were charging

00:28:23.020 --> 00:28:25.270 align:middle line:90%
close to the minimum price.

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:28.870 align:middle line:84%
Then, if you're a
firm and you charge

00:28:28.870 --> 00:28:34.030 align:middle line:84%
close to the minimum price, you
have 1 over alpha N probability

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:36.280 align:middle line:90%
of being the lowest firm.

00:28:36.280 --> 00:28:38.290 align:middle line:90%
But then because they're also--

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:40.480 align:middle line:84%
because there are, like,
alpha times N firms here,

00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:42.020 align:middle line:90%
there are a lot of firms here.

00:28:42.020 --> 00:28:44.240 align:middle line:84%
The distance between
them is very, very small.

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:46.640 align:middle line:84%
So the price sensitivity
is very, very large.

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:49.600 align:middle line:84%
So this point mass actually
has to collapse towards--

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:52.480 align:middle line:84%
this thing becomes
very close to c.

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:57.070 align:middle line:84%
And so what you basically get,
you get 1 over N consumers,

00:28:57.070 --> 00:29:01.780 align:middle line:84%
and you get a 1 over
N markup on them.

00:29:01.780 --> 00:29:04.453 align:middle line:84%
And so you'd be getting
1 over N squared profits.

00:29:04.453 --> 00:29:10.240 align:middle line:90%


00:29:10.240 --> 00:29:12.190 align:middle line:90%
And that's not going to work.

00:29:12.190 --> 00:29:14.560 align:middle line:84%
Because if you had 1
over N squared profits,

00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:18.130 align:middle line:84%
you could get 1 over N kind of
profits by just pricing up here

00:29:18.130 --> 00:29:21.220 align:middle line:84%
and getting the people
with the high s's.

00:29:21.220 --> 00:29:24.130 align:middle line:84%
And so it turns out that
the equilibrium just

00:29:24.130 --> 00:29:27.700 align:middle line:84%
is a very, very small point mass
here and a very, very big one

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:28.980 align:middle line:90%
up there, when N gets big.

00:29:28.980 --> 00:29:35.640 align:middle line:90%


00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:37.072 align:middle line:90%
So anyway, let's see.

00:29:37.072 --> 00:29:39.280 align:middle line:84%
I guess I didn't actually
say much about the theorem.

00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:42.280 align:middle line:84%
All I said is that it
doesn't have a pure strategy

00:29:42.280 --> 00:29:43.130 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:29:43.130 --> 00:29:45.875 align:middle line:84%
It does have a mixed
strategy equilibrium.

00:29:45.875 --> 00:29:47.500 align:middle line:84%
And in this mixed
strategy equilibrium,

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:49.630 align:middle line:84%
you get something
that looks like--

00:29:49.630 --> 00:29:52.807 align:middle line:84%
I just said, it has p
lower bar greater than c.

00:29:52.807 --> 00:29:54.140 align:middle line:90%
I didn't say much else about it.

00:29:54.140 --> 00:29:56.320 align:middle line:90%
It's got a density.

00:29:56.320 --> 00:29:58.960 align:middle line:90%
Why does that happen?

00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:01.760 align:middle line:84%
The argument for part a,
that it's no pure strategy

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:04.630 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium, is
just like Diamond's.

00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:08.620 align:middle line:84%
It's like Diamond's, most
of the time, that you

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:10.120 align:middle line:90%
can't have an equilibrium.

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:13.150 align:middle line:84%
Again, suppose we tried
to have an equilibrium

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:15.610 align:middle line:84%
where someone was at
$35, someone was at $36,

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:19.390 align:middle line:84%
and then some other
people were up here.

00:30:19.390 --> 00:30:22.160 align:middle line:84%
You're the single lowest
person in this model.

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:25.300 align:middle line:84%
You strictly want to increase
your price in epsilon overcut.

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:29.110 align:middle line:84%
Because, again, if consumers
expect this distribution,

00:30:29.110 --> 00:30:32.080 align:middle line:84%
you're epsilon more than that,
you still get all the shoppers,

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:35.000 align:middle line:84%
and all the nonshoppers
still buy from you.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:36.815 align:middle line:84%
And so then the
only new case is,

00:30:36.815 --> 00:30:38.690 align:middle line:84%
well, let's suppose we
had-- like in Diamond,

00:30:38.690 --> 00:30:40.220 align:middle line:84%
we have a whole
bunch of firms all

00:30:40.220 --> 00:30:43.670 align:middle line:84%
tied at exactly the same price,
whether that's the monopoly

00:30:43.670 --> 00:30:45.110 align:middle line:90%
price or something else.

00:30:45.110 --> 00:30:46.910 align:middle line:84%
Well, then you get
the Bertrand argument

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:50.480 align:middle line:84%
that you would epsilon undercut
and get all the shoppers.

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:52.730 align:middle line:84%
So we have the same
effect as Diamond, saying,

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:55.250 align:middle line:84%
you can't have people
different from each other

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:57.380 align:middle line:84%
because the lowest
firm always moves up.

00:30:57.380 --> 00:30:58.428 align:middle line:90%
And then you have a new--

00:30:58.428 --> 00:31:00.470 align:middle line:84%
and then you go back to
the Bertrand argument of,

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:02.480 align:middle line:84%
you can't have everyone
the same at anything

00:31:02.480 --> 00:31:05.000 align:middle line:84%
other than c because someone
at the bottom goes down.

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:07.160 align:middle line:84%
Someone will go down and
steal all the shoppers

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:09.890 align:middle line:84%
instead of splitting the
shoppers with the other firms.

00:31:09.890 --> 00:31:13.690 align:middle line:84%
So there's not going to be a
pure strategy equilibrium here.

00:31:13.690 --> 00:31:18.840 align:middle line:90%


00:31:18.840 --> 00:31:20.740 align:middle line:84%
What about mixed
strategy equilibria?

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:22.898 align:middle line:90%
Well, in-- yes.

00:31:22.898 --> 00:31:23.940 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Quick question.

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:25.750 align:middle line:84%
How does the shopper's
utility work?

00:31:25.750 --> 00:31:29.130 align:middle line:84%
Because if they get positive
utility from opening doors,

00:31:29.130 --> 00:31:31.807 align:middle line:84%
can't they just do that forever
and get arbitrarily large--

00:31:31.807 --> 00:31:32.640 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: Yeah.

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:34.290 align:middle line:84%
So I guess what I
have to do is I have

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:36.900 align:middle line:84%
to have my shoppers-- you only
get s the first time you open

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:37.410 align:middle line:90%
a door.

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:40.620 align:middle line:90%
You don't-- just--

00:31:40.620 --> 00:31:41.670 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:31:41.670 --> 00:31:43.420 align:middle line:84%
Once a door's opened,
it's already opened.

00:31:43.420 --> 00:31:47.280 align:middle line:84%
You don't get new utility from
opening it again, I guess.

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:49.300 align:middle line:90%
Again, it's a little--

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:51.540 align:middle line:84%
it's mostly a construct to
say that the shoppers just

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:52.837 align:middle line:90%
open every door.

00:31:52.837 --> 00:31:54.420 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, I guess
the way people also

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:57.600 align:middle line:84%
think about these models
is you may have people

00:31:57.600 --> 00:32:00.480 align:middle line:84%
who know how to use some search
engine that just gives them

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:04.650 align:middle line:90%
all the prices instant--

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:06.280 align:middle line:84%
one search gives
them all the prices,

00:32:06.280 --> 00:32:08.660 align:middle line:84%
and that's where the
shoppers are supposed to be.

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:11.340 align:middle line:90%


00:32:11.340 --> 00:32:15.180 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, in
[14.]122, I talk about,

00:32:15.180 --> 00:32:19.150 align:middle line:84%
this is a game with
discontinuous--

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:22.270 align:middle line:84%
profits do move
discontinuously in price

00:32:22.270 --> 00:32:24.860 align:middle line:84%
when you cross where another
firm is in a search model.

00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:28.480 align:middle line:84%
But Dasgupta Maskin's
existence theorem

00:32:28.480 --> 00:32:32.032 align:middle line:84%
for discontinuous games, the sum
of the payoffs is continuous.

00:32:32.032 --> 00:32:33.490 align:middle line:84%
So this is a classic
example, where

00:32:33.490 --> 00:32:35.115 align:middle line:84%
we know from general
existence theorems

00:32:35.115 --> 00:32:36.890 align:middle line:84%
that there must be
some equilibrium.

00:32:36.890 --> 00:32:39.195 align:middle line:84%
It's not pure, therefore
it must be mixed.

00:32:39.195 --> 00:32:42.400 align:middle line:90%


00:32:42.400 --> 00:32:45.530 align:middle line:84%
So we've got, some mixed
equilibrium has to exist.

00:32:45.530 --> 00:32:50.530 align:middle line:84%
If you think about firms
mixing over some interval

00:32:50.530 --> 00:32:54.490 align:middle line:84%
p lower bar, p upper bar,
could p lower bar be c?

00:32:54.490 --> 00:32:57.340 align:middle line:84%
p lower bar can't be c,
because if p lower bar

00:32:57.340 --> 00:32:59.320 align:middle line:84%
were c, firms
charging p lower bar

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:01.870 align:middle line:90%
would be earning zero profits.

00:33:01.870 --> 00:33:03.820 align:middle line:84%
If you're earning zero
profits, again, you

00:33:03.820 --> 00:33:05.990 align:middle line:90%
can go to c plus epsilon.

00:33:05.990 --> 00:33:07.720 align:middle line:84%
Anyone who visits
you first would

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:09.670 align:middle line:84%
have to buy at c
plus epsilon, so you

00:33:09.670 --> 00:33:11.420 align:middle line:90%
would get positive profits.

00:33:11.420 --> 00:33:13.810 align:middle line:84%
So this equilibrium has to
have a positive-- it has to be

00:33:13.810 --> 00:33:15.710 align:middle line:90%
a positive profit equilibrium.

00:33:15.710 --> 00:33:21.340 align:middle line:84%
So p lower bar is going to have
to be strictly bigger than c.

00:33:21.340 --> 00:33:26.290 align:middle line:84%
And then why is it a
continuous distribution

00:33:26.290 --> 00:33:28.700 align:middle line:84%
instead of a distribution
that has maybe a point mass?

00:33:28.700 --> 00:33:31.690 align:middle line:84%
There's a large mass of
firms who charge here.

00:33:31.690 --> 00:33:34.813 align:middle line:84%
Well, again, this is like an
epsilon undercutting argument.

00:33:34.813 --> 00:33:37.480 align:middle line:84%
If there was a large point mass
of firms who all charged exactly

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:40.790 align:middle line:90%
$37, you would go to $36.99.

00:33:40.790 --> 00:33:45.310 align:middle line:84%
And if in the realization
of the randomizations,

00:33:45.310 --> 00:33:49.740 align:middle line:84%
everyone randomized to $37,
then by going to $36.99,

00:33:49.740 --> 00:33:52.240 align:middle line:84%
you increase the probability
that you're the low-priced firm

00:33:52.240 --> 00:33:53.690 align:middle line:90%
and win the shoppers.

00:33:53.690 --> 00:33:56.050 align:middle line:84%
So you can't have a
point mass because that

00:33:56.050 --> 00:33:59.270 align:middle line:84%
would create an incentive to
epsilon undercut the point mass.

00:33:59.270 --> 00:34:01.683 align:middle line:84%
So it's got to be some
continuous distribution

00:34:01.683 --> 00:34:02.183 align:middle line:90%
of prices.

00:34:02.183 --> 00:34:08.749 align:middle line:90%


00:34:08.749 --> 00:34:09.920 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on that?

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:14.270 align:middle line:90%


00:34:14.270 --> 00:34:18.080 align:middle line:84%
So what does the
density look like?

00:34:18.080 --> 00:34:21.199 align:middle line:90%
The one case where it's--

00:34:21.199 --> 00:34:24.290 align:middle line:90%
the model can be hard to solve.

00:34:24.290 --> 00:34:28.040 align:middle line:84%
One case where the
model is easier to solve

00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:32.060 align:middle line:84%
is I look at a special
case where I just

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:35.570 align:middle line:90%
have a two-type version.

00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:38.550 align:middle line:84%
So I have this two-type
version of search costs.

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:44.900 align:middle line:84%
I have this fraction mu,
have s prime less than 0,

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:48.469 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going have the fraction
1 minus mu have some number s

00:34:48.469 --> 00:34:50.120 align:middle line:90%
that's greater than 0.

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:53.300 align:middle line:84%
So instead of having
the heterogeneous s's

00:34:53.300 --> 00:34:55.250 align:middle line:84%
on a positive
interval, let me just

00:34:55.250 --> 00:34:57.800 align:middle line:90%
make them all exactly the same.

00:34:57.800 --> 00:34:59.510 align:middle line:84%
I think it's a worse
model descriptively,

00:34:59.510 --> 00:35:02.750 align:middle line:84%
but it's an easier
model to solve.

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:04.670 align:middle line:90%
So what happens there?

00:35:04.670 --> 00:35:08.090 align:middle line:84%
Well, one observation is,
what are the profits if you

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:10.792 align:middle line:90%
charge price p lower bar?

00:35:10.792 --> 00:35:14.310 align:middle line:84%
Oh, actually, sorry,
first observation.

00:35:14.310 --> 00:35:15.600 align:middle line:90%
Suppose I go to this.

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:22.140 align:middle line:84%
And why do I go to this
two-type distribution?

00:35:22.140 --> 00:35:24.120 align:middle line:84%
So I go to this
two-type distribution

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:28.080 align:middle line:84%
because, when you have
the two-type distribution,

00:35:28.080 --> 00:35:31.980 align:middle line:84%
every nonshopper, every
consumer with a positive s

00:35:31.980 --> 00:35:36.000 align:middle line:84%
has to buy from the first
firm that they visit.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:38.560 align:middle line:84%
Why do they have to buy from
the first firm that they visit?

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:41.610 align:middle line:84%
Well, suppose the first
firm that they visit

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:44.940 align:middle line:84%
has price p upper
bar and you ask, OK,

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:48.450 align:middle line:84%
I know that this is the worst
price that any firm in the world

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:50.260 align:middle line:90%
offers, and I just found it.

00:35:50.260 --> 00:35:53.130 align:middle line:84%
Do I buy from it or do I
look for a better price?

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:57.090 align:middle line:84%
If everybody has the same s, the
answer is yes, you buy anyway.

00:35:57.090 --> 00:35:58.710 align:middle line:84%
Because if the
answer was no, you

00:35:58.710 --> 00:36:01.680 align:middle line:84%
don't buy if you find
the worst possible price,

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:05.190 align:middle line:84%
then the firm who's pricing at
p upper bar gets zero customers

00:36:05.190 --> 00:36:07.590 align:middle line:90%
and is earning zero profits.

00:36:07.590 --> 00:36:13.770 align:middle line:84%
So if we're in an
equilibrium, it

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:18.210 align:middle line:84%
must be that the
distribution has shrunk down

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:23.970 align:middle line:84%
to the width where if I find--
even if I find p upper bar,

00:36:23.970 --> 00:36:26.730 align:middle line:84%
it must be that the consumer
surplus from finding

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:30.570 align:middle line:84%
p lower bar minus the consumer
surplus from finding p upper bar

00:36:30.570 --> 00:36:32.370 align:middle line:90%
is at most s.

00:36:32.370 --> 00:36:37.438 align:middle line:84%
Because if that wasn't true, no
one would buy at p upper bar,

00:36:37.438 --> 00:36:39.730 align:middle line:84%
and the p upper bar firm
would be earning zero profits.

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:41.800 align:middle line:84%
So it must be the distribution
just shrinks down.

00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:45.805 align:middle line:84%
If s is small, maybe it shrinks
down to something very tight.

00:36:45.805 --> 00:36:48.180 align:middle line:84%
But it's got to be that this
equilibrium distribution has

00:36:48.180 --> 00:36:50.940 align:middle line:84%
to shrink in such a way that
people always buy when they

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:53.910 align:middle line:90%
find the p upper bar price.

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:56.490 align:middle line:84%
And so if everyone buys when
they find the p upper bar price,

00:36:56.490 --> 00:37:02.670 align:middle line:84%
then we have these guys in
equilibrium, these search all N.

00:37:02.670 --> 00:37:06.210 align:middle line:84%
And these guys only
search one firm,

00:37:06.210 --> 00:37:11.220 align:middle line:84%
and they buy if p is less
than or equal to p upper bar.

00:37:11.220 --> 00:37:13.710 align:middle line:84%
So this makes it easier to
write down the profit functions

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:16.680 align:middle line:84%
because we've gone from a
complicated search problem

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:18.790 align:middle line:84%
to some consumers are
buying from all N firms

00:37:18.790 --> 00:37:20.790 align:middle line:84%
and some consumers are
just looking at one place

00:37:20.790 --> 00:37:23.520 align:middle line:84%
and buying there, as long
as the price isn't above

00:37:23.520 --> 00:37:24.320 align:middle line:90%
what was expected.

00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:26.910 align:middle line:90%


00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:28.590 align:middle line:84%
So what do you get
as profits if you

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:30.180 align:middle line:84%
buy-- if you find
the p lower bar--

00:37:30.180 --> 00:37:32.730 align:middle line:84%
if you find the p
lower bar-- sorry.

00:37:32.730 --> 00:37:34.780 align:middle line:84%
If you're a firm, you
set price p lower bar,

00:37:34.780 --> 00:37:35.920 align:middle line:90%
what's your profits?

00:37:35.920 --> 00:37:39.060 align:middle line:84%
It's p lower bar minus
c D of p lower bar

00:37:39.060 --> 00:37:41.100 align:middle line:84%
times the number of
consumers you get,

00:37:41.100 --> 00:37:44.250 align:middle line:84%
which is all the shoppers
and a 1 over N share

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:45.370 align:middle line:90%
of the nonshoppers.

00:37:45.370 --> 00:37:47.370 align:middle line:84%
Because if the nonshoppers
go to any other firm,

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:48.578 align:middle line:90%
they will buy from that firm.

00:37:48.578 --> 00:37:51.518 align:middle line:90%


00:37:51.518 --> 00:37:54.600 align:middle line:84%
But now in a mixed
equilibrium, firms

00:37:54.600 --> 00:37:57.500 align:middle line:84%
have to be indifferent over all
the prices they're mixing over.

00:37:57.500 --> 00:38:02.280 align:middle line:84%
So that means that at any other
price, price minus cost times D

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:05.580 align:middle line:84%
of p times the number of
consumers that you get,

00:38:05.580 --> 00:38:09.600 align:middle line:84%
which is you get the
people who visit you first

00:38:09.600 --> 00:38:14.700 align:middle line:84%
and you get the shoppers, mu,
if every other firm happens

00:38:14.700 --> 00:38:16.710 align:middle line:90%
to choose a price above p.

00:38:16.710 --> 00:38:19.350 align:middle line:84%
And that happens with
probability 1 minus capital F

00:38:19.350 --> 00:38:22.500 align:middle line:90%
of p to the N minus 1 power.

00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:25.710 align:middle line:84%
So this is my profit
if I set a price of p.

00:38:25.710 --> 00:38:28.710 align:middle line:84%
This is my profit if I set
a price of p lower bar.

00:38:28.710 --> 00:38:30.870 align:middle line:90%
These have to be equal.

00:38:30.870 --> 00:38:35.490 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at this
equation, the capital F of p

00:38:35.490 --> 00:38:37.570 align:middle line:90%
appears in exactly one place.

00:38:37.570 --> 00:38:38.880 align:middle line:90%
So what can I say?

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:43.440 align:middle line:84%
First thing I can say is that I
divide both sides by p minus c

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:46.320 align:middle line:84%
D of p, and I get
mu times this equals

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:50.070 align:middle line:90%
this over p minus c D of p.

00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:54.000 align:middle line:84%
And then I can subtract 1 minus
mu over N from both sides.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:56.970 align:middle line:84%
And then I can divide
both sides by mu,

00:38:56.970 --> 00:38:59.460 align:middle line:84%
and then I've got 1
minus f of p to the N

00:38:59.460 --> 00:39:02.170 align:middle line:84%
minus 1 all alone on one
side and stuff on the other.

00:39:02.170 --> 00:39:05.160 align:middle line:84%
And then I take the N minus
first root of both sides,

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:07.630 align:middle line:84%
and then I get 1 minus
F of p equals something.

00:39:07.630 --> 00:39:09.780 align:middle line:84%
And so I can solve
this for F of p.

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:11.430 align:middle line:84%
So this is an
equation we're just

00:39:11.430 --> 00:39:13.490 align:middle line:90%
F of p appears exactly once.

00:39:13.490 --> 00:39:15.830 align:middle line:90%
So I get F of p.

00:39:15.830 --> 00:39:19.430 align:middle line:84%
I can always solve for what F of
p must be, given any p lower bar

00:39:19.430 --> 00:39:23.340 align:middle line:84%
and given any mu and all
those other parameters.

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:26.418 align:middle line:84%
And then what is p
upper bar going to be?

00:39:26.418 --> 00:39:27.960 align:middle line:84%
p upper bar is going
to have to solve

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:30.990 align:middle line:84%
this-- p minus c D of p
upper bar equals 1 minus mu

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:33.780 align:middle line:90%
over N times this.

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:37.470 align:middle line:84%
And so then I can also use
that to figure out p upper bar

00:39:37.470 --> 00:39:40.770 align:middle line:84%
is a point where the
revenue is equal to this

00:39:40.770 --> 00:39:43.590 align:middle line:90%
divided by something or other.

00:39:43.590 --> 00:39:46.810 align:middle line:84%
So if p lower bar is too
big, that won't exist.

00:39:46.810 --> 00:39:49.350 align:middle line:84%
But if p lower bar is
small, that will exist.

00:39:49.350 --> 00:39:53.850 align:middle line:84%
And you're going to find that
I plug in a p lower bar--

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:57.150 align:middle line:84%
say, I plug in a guess
of what p lower bar is.

00:39:57.150 --> 00:39:59.040 align:middle line:84%
And then given that p
lower bar, I say, OK,

00:39:59.040 --> 00:40:01.560 align:middle line:84%
if that's the p lower bar,
this is what the distribution

00:40:01.560 --> 00:40:03.210 align:middle line:90%
is going to look like.

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:06.410 align:middle line:84%
And this is what p upper
bar would have to be.

00:40:06.410 --> 00:40:08.270 align:middle line:84%
So for any guess
of p lower bar, I

00:40:08.270 --> 00:40:11.785 align:middle line:84%
can solve for what does
capital F look like

00:40:11.785 --> 00:40:13.160 align:middle line:84%
and what does
little f look like?

00:40:13.160 --> 00:40:15.180 align:middle line:84%
And where do we run
out of probability?

00:40:15.180 --> 00:40:17.020 align:middle line:90%
And it has to equal 1 up here.

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:21.450 align:middle line:90%


00:40:21.450 --> 00:40:25.380 align:middle line:84%
So is that thing
that I solve for--

00:40:25.380 --> 00:40:27.990 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be a complicated
formula, with something

00:40:27.990 --> 00:40:29.470 align:middle line:90%
to the 1 over N minus 1 power.

00:40:29.470 --> 00:40:31.740 align:middle line:90%
Is that an equilibrium?

00:40:31.740 --> 00:40:36.630 align:middle line:84%
I can always make people
indifferent over all prices

00:40:36.630 --> 00:40:38.880 align:middle line:84%
in the interval p
lower bar, p upper bar,

00:40:38.880 --> 00:40:42.480 align:middle line:84%
but that's not sufficient to
show that it's an equilibrium.

00:40:42.480 --> 00:40:44.280 align:middle line:84%
Because what I have
to show is that I've

00:40:44.280 --> 00:40:47.140 align:middle line:84%
made the firms by construction
in different overall prices.

00:40:47.140 --> 00:40:50.430 align:middle line:84%
In this interval, I have to show
that they don't prefer pricing

00:40:50.430 --> 00:40:52.590 align:middle line:90%
lower or pricing higher.

00:40:52.590 --> 00:40:55.260 align:middle line:84%
The constraint that turns
out to be hard to satisfy

00:40:55.260 --> 00:40:57.765 align:middle line:84%
is the price that they
don't want to price higher.

00:40:57.765 --> 00:41:00.900 align:middle line:90%


00:41:00.900 --> 00:41:02.730 align:middle line:90%
Why set price p upper bar?

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:07.740 align:middle line:84%
Why not go to p upper bar plus
epsilon or all the way up to pm?

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:11.403 align:middle line:84%
Because, again, you know
that there's zero probability

00:41:11.403 --> 00:41:14.070 align:middle line:84%
you're going to get the shoppers
if you're charging p upper bar.

00:41:14.070 --> 00:41:17.100 align:middle line:84%
You're only going to get the
people who open your door first.

00:41:17.100 --> 00:41:18.990 align:middle line:84%
Why doesn't it then make
sense to open your--

00:41:18.990 --> 00:41:21.490 align:middle line:84%
given that you only get the
people who open your door first,

00:41:21.490 --> 00:41:23.530 align:middle line:90%
why not go epsilon higher?

00:41:23.530 --> 00:41:26.510 align:middle line:84%
And there could be two reasons
why you don't go higher.

00:41:26.510 --> 00:41:29.860 align:middle line:84%
One could be that p
upper bar that you

00:41:29.860 --> 00:41:32.020 align:middle line:90%
solved for was equal to pm.

00:41:32.020 --> 00:41:36.910 align:middle line:84%
If the p upper bar was
equal to pm, then obviously

00:41:36.910 --> 00:41:39.640 align:middle line:84%
you don't want to
go epsilon higher.

00:41:39.640 --> 00:41:41.230 align:middle line:84%
The other thing
that could happen

00:41:41.230 --> 00:41:48.130 align:middle line:84%
is, when consumers see price p
upper bar, the gain that they

00:41:48.130 --> 00:41:49.810 align:middle line:84%
would get from
searching again-- again,

00:41:49.810 --> 00:41:54.190 align:middle line:84%
the gain that they would get
from searching again is this.

00:41:54.190 --> 00:41:56.633 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, it's not that.

00:41:56.633 --> 00:41:57.550 align:middle line:90%
I will say what it is.

00:41:57.550 --> 00:42:03.820 align:middle line:84%
But if consumers--
it's going to be the--

00:42:03.820 --> 00:42:04.940 align:middle line:90%
it's on the next slide.

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:07.270 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be the
integral from p lower bar

00:42:07.270 --> 00:42:13.000 align:middle line:84%
to p upper bar of consumer
surplus of p minus consumer

00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:19.990 align:middle line:84%
surplus at p upper
bar F of p dp.

00:42:19.990 --> 00:42:24.250 align:middle line:90%
If I go to search again--

00:42:24.250 --> 00:42:26.860 align:middle line:84%
I'll call this thing
v of p upper bar.

00:42:26.860 --> 00:42:30.830 align:middle line:84%
If I search again, I may find
a lower price than p upper bar.

00:42:30.830 --> 00:42:34.150 align:middle line:84%
And if I do, I gain
more consumer surplus.

00:42:34.150 --> 00:42:37.610 align:middle line:84%
And so this integral would tell
me what is my gain from search.

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:43.450 align:middle line:84%
So it could be that v of p
upper bar is exactly equal to s.

00:42:43.450 --> 00:42:45.790 align:middle line:84%
And if v of p upper
bar is equal to s,

00:42:45.790 --> 00:42:48.470 align:middle line:84%
everybody who finds p
upper bar would buy.

00:42:48.470 --> 00:42:50.350 align:middle line:84%
But if you saw p upper
bar plus epsilon,

00:42:50.350 --> 00:42:51.560 align:middle line:90%
you'd be, that's ridiculous.

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:54.520 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to go take a new draw
from the price distribution.

00:42:54.520 --> 00:42:56.230 align:middle line:84%
So what we have
to do, basically,

00:42:56.230 --> 00:43:00.590 align:middle line:84%
is look for a p lower bar that
makes one of two things true.

00:43:00.590 --> 00:43:03.800 align:middle line:84%
Either it makes p
upper bar equal to pm

00:43:03.800 --> 00:43:09.380 align:middle line:84%
or it makes v of p
upper bar equal to s.

00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:10.970 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, to find
the distribution,

00:43:10.970 --> 00:43:12.170 align:middle line:84%
I just search for
a p lower bar that

00:43:12.170 --> 00:43:13.510 align:middle line:90%
does one of those two things.

00:43:13.510 --> 00:43:16.020 align:middle line:90%


00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:23.075 align:middle line:84%
This one is actually not hard to
find because, if you look at it,

00:43:23.075 --> 00:43:25.210 align:middle line:84%
we're going to have
this equation is true.

00:43:25.210 --> 00:43:26.960 align:middle line:84%
So I could just plug
in the monopoly price

00:43:26.960 --> 00:43:29.990 align:middle line:84%
here and put monopoly
price minus c D

00:43:29.990 --> 00:43:32.570 align:middle line:90%
of pm times this equals this.

00:43:32.570 --> 00:43:34.070 align:middle line:84%
And so the only
thing that's unknown

00:43:34.070 --> 00:43:35.490 align:middle line:90%
is the revenue at p lower bar.

00:43:35.490 --> 00:43:37.573 align:middle line:84%
And so I can solve for the
revenue at p lower bar.

00:43:37.573 --> 00:43:39.170 align:middle line:90%
I can find p lower bar.

00:43:39.170 --> 00:43:41.253 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, you
find an equilibrium

00:43:41.253 --> 00:43:42.920 align:middle line:84%
by looking for something
that solves one

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:44.446 align:middle line:90%
of these two extra conditions.

00:43:44.446 --> 00:43:47.950 align:middle line:90%


00:43:47.950 --> 00:43:49.830 align:middle line:84%
Questions on heterogeneous
search models?

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:53.800 align:middle line:90%


00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:55.480 align:middle line:90%
Value of Price Search.

00:43:55.480 --> 00:43:59.890 align:middle line:84%
I put this slide in because this
is a "read it yourself at home."

00:43:59.890 --> 00:44:03.190 align:middle line:84%
But just this is
the way you normally

00:44:03.190 --> 00:44:04.960 align:middle line:84%
think of the value
of one price quote.

00:44:04.960 --> 00:44:06.585 align:middle line:84%
The value of getting
a new price quote,

00:44:06.585 --> 00:44:08.170 align:middle line:84%
if you've already
found a price p,

00:44:08.170 --> 00:44:10.780 align:middle line:84%
is the integral from
p lower bar to p,

00:44:10.780 --> 00:44:15.710 align:middle line:84%
consumer surplus at x minus
consumer surplus of p f of x dx.

00:44:15.710 --> 00:44:17.530 align:middle line:84%
That's the natural
representation

00:44:17.530 --> 00:44:20.020 align:middle line:84%
of what getting one more
price quote gets me.

00:44:20.020 --> 00:44:22.210 align:middle line:84%
It gets me what I gain
if I find the lower price

00:44:22.210 --> 00:44:26.140 align:middle line:84%
quote times the density of
the prices I'm drawing from.

00:44:26.140 --> 00:44:28.130 align:middle line:84%
Something you see
often when you're

00:44:28.130 --> 00:44:31.120 align:middle line:84%
redoing this, when you're
working at papers, is people

00:44:31.120 --> 00:44:33.350 align:middle line:84%
often write v of p is
the integral from p

00:44:33.350 --> 00:44:39.070 align:middle line:84%
lower bar to p D of x F
of x-- capital F of x dx.

00:44:39.070 --> 00:44:41.620 align:middle line:84%
You can get from here to
here by integrating by parts.

00:44:41.620 --> 00:44:43.120 align:middle line:84%
You can also get
from there to there

00:44:43.120 --> 00:44:45.260 align:middle line:84%
by reversing the
order of integration.

00:44:45.260 --> 00:44:47.917 align:middle line:84%
People often write down
this less-intuitive formula,

00:44:47.917 --> 00:44:49.750 align:middle line:84%
just because it's
well-known, without noting

00:44:49.750 --> 00:44:50.660 align:middle line:90%
why they're doing it.

00:44:50.660 --> 00:44:54.610 align:middle line:84%
So I put it on a slide
and put demonstration

00:44:54.610 --> 00:44:56.110 align:middle line:84%
of flipping the
order of integration

00:44:56.110 --> 00:44:58.880 align:middle line:84%
to prove those two
things are equivalent.

00:44:58.880 --> 00:45:05.250 align:middle line:90%


00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:09.180 align:middle line:84%
So second thing I
want to do is search

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:10.830 align:middle line:90%
with differentiated products.

00:45:10.830 --> 00:45:15.060 align:middle line:84%
I said that we want search costs
because we think differentiation

00:45:15.060 --> 00:45:16.530 align:middle line:84%
is not sufficient
to explain what

00:45:16.530 --> 00:45:18.910 align:middle line:84%
goes on in the world
with real-world markups.

00:45:18.910 --> 00:45:21.510 align:middle line:84%
But that doesn't mean
differentiation is not real.

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:26.010 align:middle line:84%
And so while I think more
people do search in that model

00:45:26.010 --> 00:45:27.930 align:middle line:84%
like I just did, do
the Bertrand search

00:45:27.930 --> 00:45:30.900 align:middle line:84%
and then figure out
what it does to markups,

00:45:30.900 --> 00:45:32.580 align:middle line:84%
it's more realistic
to have search

00:45:32.580 --> 00:45:35.070 align:middle line:84%
added on top of a
differentiated product model

00:45:35.070 --> 00:45:37.440 align:middle line:84%
rather than searching
a Bertrand model.

00:45:37.440 --> 00:45:40.050 align:middle line:84%
So that's what I'm going
to show for you here.

00:45:40.050 --> 00:45:44.340 align:middle line:84%
So now, I'm going
to have two firms

00:45:44.340 --> 00:45:48.030 align:middle line:90%
with constant marginal cost c.

00:45:48.030 --> 00:45:50.700 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to do
the Perloff-Salop

00:45:50.700 --> 00:45:55.860 align:middle line:84%
version of Hotelling's
model, where consumers

00:45:55.860 --> 00:45:57.515 align:middle line:90%
have a two-dimensional type.

00:45:57.515 --> 00:46:02.470 align:middle line:90%


00:46:02.470 --> 00:46:06.350 align:middle line:90%
So consumers have a--

00:46:06.350 --> 00:46:09.380 align:middle line:90%
yeah, so they have a theta--

00:46:09.380 --> 00:46:14.540 align:middle line:84%
consumer i has a theta i1,
and they have a theta i2.

00:46:14.540 --> 00:46:19.220 align:middle line:84%
The theta i1 is related to how
much they like firm 1's product.

00:46:19.220 --> 00:46:23.450 align:middle line:84%
The theta i2 is related to how
much they like firm 2's product.

00:46:23.450 --> 00:46:27.380 align:middle line:84%
I guess here, I did it with a
v minus t, so this is your--

00:46:27.380 --> 00:46:30.650 align:middle line:90%


00:46:30.650 --> 00:46:33.540 align:middle line:84%
some people find
both firms ideal.

00:46:33.540 --> 00:46:35.650 align:middle line:84%
They're the ones who
have theta is equal to 0.

00:46:35.650 --> 00:46:39.450 align:middle line:84%
Some consumers really like firm
2, and they don't like firm 1.

00:46:39.450 --> 00:46:40.550 align:middle line:90%
They're down here.

00:46:40.550 --> 00:46:43.260 align:middle line:84%
Some consumers really like
firm 1, don't like firm 2.

00:46:43.260 --> 00:46:44.250 align:middle line:90%
They're up here.

00:46:44.250 --> 00:46:48.260 align:middle line:84%
Some consumers really find both
products far from their tastes.

00:46:48.260 --> 00:46:51.500 align:middle line:84%
So it's the Perloff-Salop
version of Hotelling,

00:46:51.500 --> 00:46:53.180 align:middle line:90%
where I just have--

00:46:53.180 --> 00:46:55.970 align:middle line:84%
instead of having the theta
i2 equals negative theta

00:46:55.970 --> 00:47:02.690 align:middle line:84%
i1 or t minus theta i1, I make
independent disutilities--

00:47:02.690 --> 00:47:08.500 align:middle line:84%
independent tastes for
product 1 and product 2.

00:47:08.500 --> 00:47:12.880 align:middle line:84%
So the firms simultaneously
choose their prices, p1, p2.

00:47:12.880 --> 00:47:18.490 align:middle line:84%
And what the consumers here have
to do now is, when they pay s,

00:47:18.490 --> 00:47:21.490 align:middle line:84%
they both learn
the price and they

00:47:21.490 --> 00:47:25.120 align:middle line:84%
learn whether they like
the product or not.

00:47:25.120 --> 00:47:29.590 align:middle line:84%
So this is the case where you
know there are two firms--

00:47:29.590 --> 00:47:32.650 align:middle line:84%
you know there are two firms
that are selling some product.

00:47:32.650 --> 00:47:35.320 align:middle line:84%
But you have to
actually go to the store

00:47:35.320 --> 00:47:38.740 align:middle line:84%
and look at the product, at
their version of the product,

00:47:38.740 --> 00:47:41.523 align:middle line:84%
to decide whether
you like it or not.

00:47:41.523 --> 00:47:42.940 align:middle line:84%
Or you could think
of it as, like,

00:47:42.940 --> 00:47:44.560 align:middle line:90%
in the credit card example.

00:47:44.560 --> 00:47:45.940 align:middle line:90%
I pay s.

00:47:45.940 --> 00:47:47.845 align:middle line:90%
When I pay s, I learn--

00:47:47.845 --> 00:47:51.010 align:middle line:90%


00:47:51.010 --> 00:47:52.810 align:middle line:84%
I learn what the
interest rate is.

00:47:52.810 --> 00:47:55.270 align:middle line:84%
But then I also learn other
features of the credit cards

00:47:55.270 --> 00:47:56.075 align:middle line:90%
that are not--

00:47:56.075 --> 00:47:58.450 align:middle line:84%
which people have horizontally
differentiated preference.

00:47:58.450 --> 00:48:00.370 align:middle line:84%
Like, I do learn
what color it is,

00:48:00.370 --> 00:48:04.300 align:middle line:84%
and I do learn what their
prepayment policy is

00:48:04.300 --> 00:48:06.610 align:middle line:84%
and what day of the week
they want their things due.

00:48:06.610 --> 00:48:10.600 align:middle line:84%
Or I go to the car
dealership, and I

00:48:10.600 --> 00:48:14.010 align:middle line:84%
do learn how hard it
is to drive that car.

00:48:14.010 --> 00:48:16.810 align:middle line:84%
I do learn how
clean is the service

00:48:16.810 --> 00:48:19.300 align:middle line:84%
department at that
store and other things

00:48:19.300 --> 00:48:20.270 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:48:20.270 --> 00:48:26.050 align:middle line:84%
So I pay my s, I
learn the price,

00:48:26.050 --> 00:48:27.860 align:middle line:84%
and I learn whether
I like it or not.

00:48:27.860 --> 00:48:30.640 align:middle line:84%
And until I go there,
my prior would be

00:48:30.640 --> 00:48:32.255 align:middle line:90%
that my theta is uniform 0, 1.

00:48:32.255 --> 00:48:34.130 align:middle line:84%
I know that this might
be a good match to me.

00:48:34.130 --> 00:48:36.280 align:middle line:84%
It might not be a good match to
me, but I don't know until I--

00:48:36.280 --> 00:48:38.030 align:middle line:84%
I have to inspect the
product to see that.

00:48:38.030 --> 00:48:41.190 align:middle line:90%


00:48:41.190 --> 00:48:44.500 align:middle line:84%
Assume that the search
cost is less than t over 2.

00:48:44.500 --> 00:48:46.170 align:middle line:84%
And again, assume
that consumers use

00:48:46.170 --> 00:48:49.470 align:middle line:84%
some optimal sequential
search procedure, which

00:48:49.470 --> 00:48:53.430 align:middle line:84%
is they go visit one firm,
they learn the theta, they then

00:48:53.430 --> 00:48:56.280 align:middle line:84%
decide, given what I found about
the price and the match quality

00:48:56.280 --> 00:48:58.728 align:middle line:84%
to my tastes, do I go
search for a second time,

00:48:58.728 --> 00:48:59.645 align:middle line:90%
and then they're done.

00:48:59.645 --> 00:49:02.340 align:middle line:90%


00:49:02.340 --> 00:49:05.760 align:middle line:84%
The theorem is, for large
enough v, this model

00:49:05.760 --> 00:49:08.850 align:middle line:84%
has a symmetric pure
strategy Nash equilibrium.

00:49:08.850 --> 00:49:12.150 align:middle line:84%
And that symmetric Nash
equilibrium is price equals c

00:49:12.150 --> 00:49:17.430 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 over 2 minus square
root of 2s over t times t.

00:49:17.430 --> 00:49:21.270 align:middle line:84%
So it's a c plus
something times t markup.

00:49:21.270 --> 00:49:23.260 align:middle line:84%
And it's a pure
strategy equilibrium.

00:49:23.260 --> 00:49:27.850 align:middle line:84%
So the Stahl-like
dispersion has gone away.

00:49:27.850 --> 00:49:31.570 align:middle line:84%
And here it's just search
cost effect markups.

00:49:31.570 --> 00:49:37.050 align:middle line:84%
So notice that the Perloff-Salop
model with this game would have

00:49:37.050 --> 00:49:39.340 align:middle line:90%
p star equals c plus 1/2 t.

00:49:39.340 --> 00:49:42.880 align:middle line:90%


00:49:42.880 --> 00:49:50.080 align:middle line:84%
Here, if you set s equals
0, you get c plus 1/2 t.

00:49:50.080 --> 00:49:54.070 align:middle line:84%
So when s goes to 0, this
goes to Perloff-Salop.

00:49:54.070 --> 00:49:58.100 align:middle line:84%
But when s is larger, like,
imagine s is t over 2--

00:49:58.100 --> 00:50:03.320 align:middle line:84%
when s is t over 2, you get
2 times t over 2 is t over t.

00:50:03.320 --> 00:50:05.680 align:middle line:90%
So it goes to c plus t.

00:50:05.680 --> 00:50:11.740 align:middle line:84%
So price increases smoothly
from c plus t over 2 to c

00:50:11.740 --> 00:50:15.400 align:middle line:90%
plus t as search costs increase.

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:20.290 align:middle line:84%
This model has the "falling
off a cliff" property that,

00:50:20.290 --> 00:50:22.840 align:middle line:84%
if you make s-- so
as s goes from--

00:50:22.840 --> 00:50:25.860 align:middle line:90%


00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:33.850 align:middle line:84%
if I graph a search cost, when s
is 0, price is c plus t over 2.

00:50:33.850 --> 00:50:43.580 align:middle line:84%
As s goes up from 0 to t over
2, it's just a 45-degree line.

00:50:43.580 --> 00:50:47.590 align:middle line:84%
Prices increase and
they reach c plus t.

00:50:47.590 --> 00:50:50.710 align:middle line:84%
Once I pass this search
cost, we fall off a cliff

00:50:50.710 --> 00:50:53.860 align:middle line:84%
and the search disappears
and no one ever buys.

00:50:53.860 --> 00:50:59.080 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is because,
when s is large enough,

00:50:59.080 --> 00:51:02.170 align:middle line:84%
no one is going to
get a second quote.

00:51:02.170 --> 00:51:04.180 align:middle line:84%
So even if you find
the first product

00:51:04.180 --> 00:51:07.030 align:middle line:84%
and it's the worst possible
product for you, and you know,

00:51:07.030 --> 00:51:10.630 align:middle line:84%
OK, I hate firm 1's
product, the most

00:51:10.630 --> 00:51:12.610 align:middle line:84%
you're going to gain
from getting firm 2's

00:51:12.610 --> 00:51:15.220 align:middle line:84%
product in expectation
is t over 2

00:51:15.220 --> 00:51:17.770 align:middle line:84%
because firm 2 is disutility--
firm 2's product might

00:51:17.770 --> 00:51:18.940 align:middle line:90%
be just as bad.

00:51:18.940 --> 00:51:20.560 align:middle line:90%
Firm 2's product might be great.

00:51:20.560 --> 00:51:23.198 align:middle line:84%
But you're only going to
gain t over 2 on average.

00:51:23.198 --> 00:51:25.240 align:middle line:84%
If you're only going to
gain t over 2 on average,

00:51:25.240 --> 00:51:28.580 align:middle line:84%
you won't do a second search if
your s is bigger than t over 2.

00:51:28.580 --> 00:51:33.700 align:middle line:84%
And so then once people
have an s bigger than that,

00:51:33.700 --> 00:51:34.880 align:middle line:90%
we're back to Diamond.

00:51:34.880 --> 00:51:36.250 align:middle line:90%
Everyone only searches once.

00:51:36.250 --> 00:51:38.000 align:middle line:90%
We go to the monopoly price.

00:51:38.000 --> 00:51:40.180 align:middle line:84%
But when we get to
the monopoly price

00:51:40.180 --> 00:51:43.900 align:middle line:84%
without the multiunit demands,
at the monopoly price,

00:51:43.900 --> 00:51:46.450 align:middle line:84%
you don't want to be--
there's no point in buying

00:51:46.450 --> 00:51:49.150 align:middle line:84%
at the monopoly price because
the monopoly price is at least v

00:51:49.150 --> 00:51:49.780 align:middle line:90%
minus t.

00:51:49.780 --> 00:51:52.400 align:middle line:84%
Your expected utility's,
at most, t over 2.

00:51:52.400 --> 00:51:54.100 align:middle line:84%
In this model, at
the monopoly price,

00:51:54.100 --> 00:51:57.667 align:middle line:84%
you wouldn't bother
searching to begin with.

00:51:57.667 --> 00:51:59.500 align:middle line:84%
Because consumer surplus,
the monopoly price

00:51:59.500 --> 00:52:01.605 align:middle line:84%
is very low in this
single-unit demand model.

00:52:01.605 --> 00:52:06.010 align:middle line:90%


00:52:06.010 --> 00:52:11.090 align:middle line:84%
So intuition for why is it that
equilibrium prices increase

00:52:11.090 --> 00:52:14.030 align:middle line:90%
in search costs?

00:52:14.030 --> 00:52:16.120 align:middle line:84%
If a firm is considering
a price increase,

00:52:16.120 --> 00:52:17.870 align:middle line:84%
they have to trade off
the gains that they

00:52:17.870 --> 00:52:20.370 align:middle line:84%
get from these inframarginal
consumers who would have bought

00:52:20.370 --> 00:52:24.920 align:middle line:84%
anyway against the losses
from raising the price

00:52:24.920 --> 00:52:28.450 align:middle line:84%
and having the marginal
consumers go away.

00:52:28.450 --> 00:52:32.500 align:middle line:90%


00:52:32.500 --> 00:52:39.550 align:middle line:84%
Here, as s gets bigger, there
are fewer marginal consumers.

00:52:39.550 --> 00:52:42.970 align:middle line:84%
When s is small, most consumers
are going to visit both--

00:52:42.970 --> 00:52:45.520 align:middle line:84%
when s is small,
I'm going to have

00:52:45.520 --> 00:52:48.910 align:middle line:84%
some region like this,
like, these consumers

00:52:48.910 --> 00:52:52.900 align:middle line:90%
buy and these search again.

00:52:52.900 --> 00:52:55.090 align:middle line:84%
Because if I'm
firm 1, for someone

00:52:55.090 --> 00:52:57.520 align:middle line:84%
to buy from me when
s is tiny, why not

00:52:57.520 --> 00:52:58.562 align:middle line:90%
get a second price quote?

00:52:58.562 --> 00:53:00.978 align:middle line:84%
It's only if they really like
me that they'll stop and buy

00:53:00.978 --> 00:53:01.600 align:middle line:90%
from me.

00:53:01.600 --> 00:53:04.780 align:middle line:84%
When s gets big,
this line shifts out,

00:53:04.780 --> 00:53:07.100 align:middle line:84%
and it becomes
these guys all buy.

00:53:07.100 --> 00:53:09.010 align:middle line:84%
And it's only if you
have a really bad draw

00:53:09.010 --> 00:53:11.230 align:middle line:90%
that you search again.

00:53:11.230 --> 00:53:17.890 align:middle line:84%
And so what happens is
just, when s gets big,

00:53:17.890 --> 00:53:21.790 align:middle line:84%
there are fewer and fewer
consumers who see both prices.

00:53:21.790 --> 00:53:24.620 align:middle line:84%
Because there are fewer
consumers who see both prices,

00:53:24.620 --> 00:53:28.490 align:middle line:84%
there's a smaller gain
to an epsilon price cut.

00:53:28.490 --> 00:53:30.820 align:middle line:84%
And so your incentive
to cut price

00:53:30.820 --> 00:53:32.870 align:middle line:84%
is smaller because there
are fewer consumers who

00:53:32.870 --> 00:53:34.840 align:middle line:90%
actually see both prices.

00:53:34.840 --> 00:53:36.533 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, you know
in equilibrium,

00:53:36.533 --> 00:53:37.950 align:middle line:84%
you're getting
half the consumers.

00:53:37.950 --> 00:53:40.325 align:middle line:84%
And on those half the consumers,
if you raise your price,

00:53:40.325 --> 00:53:42.560 align:middle line:84%
you're going to
get more on all--

00:53:42.560 --> 00:53:44.280 align:middle line:90%
on roughly half of the people.

00:53:44.280 --> 00:53:47.162 align:middle line:84%
So the number of marginal
consumers goes down.

00:53:47.162 --> 00:53:49.370 align:middle line:84%
The number of consumers
you're getting in equilibrium

00:53:49.370 --> 00:53:50.580 align:middle line:90%
stays the same.

00:53:50.580 --> 00:53:53.570 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, your profit
on the marginal consumers

00:53:53.570 --> 00:53:56.450 align:middle line:84%
must be going up to make
you not want to lose them.

00:53:56.450 --> 00:53:59.540 align:middle line:84%
And so that's an intuition
for why the markups have

00:53:59.540 --> 00:54:00.837 align:middle line:90%
to go up because you--

00:54:00.837 --> 00:54:02.420 align:middle line:84%
the markups have to
go up to the point

00:54:02.420 --> 00:54:03.980 align:middle line:84%
where you don't want to
lose that dwindling number

00:54:03.980 --> 00:54:05.146 align:middle line:90%
of marginal consumers.

00:54:05.146 --> 00:54:07.836 align:middle line:90%


00:54:07.836 --> 00:54:09.205 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on that?

00:54:09.205 --> 00:54:12.820 align:middle line:90%


00:54:12.820 --> 00:54:15.183 align:middle line:90%
I'm trying to think.

00:54:15.183 --> 00:54:16.850 align:middle line:84%
Why don't I go over
this in a little bit

00:54:16.850 --> 00:54:19.460 align:middle line:84%
of detail, just how the
calculation in a model like this

00:54:19.460 --> 00:54:20.130 align:middle line:90%
works.

00:54:20.130 --> 00:54:25.070 align:middle line:84%
So imagine in this model
you've got a consumer.

00:54:25.070 --> 00:54:27.170 align:middle line:84%
I switched from
the theta dimension

00:54:27.170 --> 00:54:29.100 align:middle line:90%
to the match quality dimension.

00:54:29.100 --> 00:54:33.950 align:middle line:84%
So imagine that a
consumer visits one store.

00:54:33.950 --> 00:54:38.000 align:middle line:84%
They find a match quality of x,
by which I mean 1 minus theta i,

00:54:38.000 --> 00:54:42.620 align:middle line:84%
and a price of p star, which is
the price they expected to get.

00:54:42.620 --> 00:54:45.290 align:middle line:84%
What's your expected
gain from search?

00:54:45.290 --> 00:54:47.540 align:middle line:84%
In equilibrium, you expect
that the other firm is also

00:54:47.540 --> 00:54:48.980 align:middle line:90%
charging p star.

00:54:48.980 --> 00:54:52.250 align:middle line:84%
So the gain comes from
getting a better match quality

00:54:52.250 --> 00:54:57.410 align:middle line:84%
at the second firm, which is
just an integral from x up to 1

00:54:57.410 --> 00:55:01.130 align:middle line:90%
of u minus x du.

00:55:01.130 --> 00:55:07.250 align:middle line:84%
So I might find a firm
that's essentially no better.

00:55:07.250 --> 00:55:10.780 align:middle line:84%
I might find a firm
that's 1 minus x better.

00:55:10.780 --> 00:55:13.960 align:middle line:84%
And I'm equally likely to
find a firm that's any-- well,

00:55:13.960 --> 00:55:17.420 align:middle line:84%
I may find a firm that's worse
than what I found already.

00:55:17.420 --> 00:55:20.020 align:middle line:84%
But if I found a firm that's
worse, I can just ignore it.

00:55:20.020 --> 00:55:22.390 align:middle line:84%
If I find something
that's the same or better,

00:55:22.390 --> 00:55:23.600 align:middle line:90%
I can buy from it.

00:55:23.600 --> 00:55:25.780 align:middle line:84%
And so there's a
1 minus x chance

00:55:25.780 --> 00:55:27.760 align:middle line:90%
that I'll find something better.

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:30.040 align:middle line:84%
The most by which I
might increase my utility

00:55:30.040 --> 00:55:32.080 align:middle line:90%
is 1 minus x times t.

00:55:32.080 --> 00:55:35.260 align:middle line:84%
And so I get this
integral, which is just--

00:55:35.260 --> 00:55:38.880 align:middle line:84%
it's the area of a triangle,
1/2 t 1 minus x squared.

00:55:38.880 --> 00:55:42.100 align:middle line:84%
So that's my gain from search
is the gain from improved match

00:55:42.100 --> 00:55:44.990 align:middle line:90%
quality.

00:55:44.990 --> 00:55:47.090 align:middle line:84%
When are you going to
do a second search?

00:55:47.090 --> 00:55:49.400 align:middle line:84%
Well, you're going to do
the second search if--

00:55:49.400 --> 00:55:51.860 align:middle line:84%
suppose I find a
match quality of x,

00:55:51.860 --> 00:55:55.530 align:middle line:84%
and I actually find a price
that's different from p1 star.

00:55:55.530 --> 00:55:59.810 align:middle line:84%
So if the firm is charging
even a higher price,

00:55:59.810 --> 00:56:01.647 align:middle line:84%
it's like the match
quality is worse.

00:56:01.647 --> 00:56:03.230 align:middle line:84%
If the firm is
charging a lower price,

00:56:03.230 --> 00:56:05.400 align:middle line:84%
it's like the match quality
that I found is better.

00:56:05.400 --> 00:56:06.780 align:middle line:90%
And so I'm going to buy--

00:56:06.780 --> 00:56:11.420 align:middle line:84%
I got to search again if v
of x plus p star minus p1

00:56:11.420 --> 00:56:15.755 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to my search cost.

00:56:15.755 --> 00:56:19.440 align:middle line:84%
In equilibrium,
p1 equals p star.

00:56:19.440 --> 00:56:23.510 align:middle line:84%
So this holds if 1/2 t 1
minus x star is less than s,

00:56:23.510 --> 00:56:27.590 align:middle line:84%
which is if and only if x is at
least 1 minus square root of 2s

00:56:27.590 --> 00:56:28.790 align:middle line:90%
over t.

00:56:28.790 --> 00:56:33.387 align:middle line:84%
I just solve this thing
for x, moving the 2 divided

00:56:33.387 --> 00:56:35.720 align:middle line:84%
by t over to the other side
and then taking square roots

00:56:35.720 --> 00:56:39.312 align:middle line:90%
and then doing 1 minus it.

00:56:39.312 --> 00:56:42.910 align:middle line:84%
So the Nash equilibrium
is always the solution

00:56:42.910 --> 00:56:45.790 align:middle line:84%
to partial pi 1
with respect to p1,

00:56:45.790 --> 00:56:50.440 align:middle line:84%
evaluated at both firms
charging p star equals 0.

00:56:50.440 --> 00:56:54.190 align:middle line:84%
And as I said, often,
if you're looking

00:56:54.190 --> 00:56:57.058 align:middle line:84%
for symmetric equilibria, rather
than writing out best responses

00:56:57.058 --> 00:56:59.350 align:middle line:84%
and intersecting them and
solving two equations and two

00:56:59.350 --> 00:57:01.225 align:middle line:84%
unknowns, you just take
advantage of the fact

00:57:01.225 --> 00:57:03.130 align:middle line:84%
that you know it's
symmetric and just do,

00:57:03.130 --> 00:57:05.140 align:middle line:84%
this first-order
condition characterizes

00:57:05.140 --> 00:57:08.500 align:middle line:84%
what the symmetric
equilibrium p star must be.

00:57:08.500 --> 00:57:11.980 align:middle line:84%
And so let's think about, well,
if p star is in equilibrium,

00:57:11.980 --> 00:57:15.970 align:middle line:84%
what happens if firm 1 cuts
its price to p star minus dp?

00:57:15.970 --> 00:57:18.730 align:middle line:84%
And let's think about, what
are the gains and losses when

00:57:18.730 --> 00:57:21.280 align:middle line:90%
I'm comparing profit at--

00:57:21.280 --> 00:57:25.750 align:middle line:84%
cutting my price to profit
from keeping my price the same?

00:57:25.750 --> 00:57:29.650 align:middle line:84%
And as I said, one loss
that I get from a price cut

00:57:29.650 --> 00:57:31.720 align:middle line:84%
is, I know in equilibrium,
half the consumers

00:57:31.720 --> 00:57:32.870 align:middle line:90%
are buying from me.

00:57:32.870 --> 00:57:36.190 align:middle line:84%
So if I cut my price by
dp, I'm giving dp away

00:57:36.190 --> 00:57:37.220 align:middle line:90%
to all those people.

00:57:37.220 --> 00:57:41.060 align:middle line:84%
That's a loss of 1/2 dp, because
I'm getting half the consumers

00:57:41.060 --> 00:57:42.560 align:middle line:90%
in equilibrium.

00:57:42.560 --> 00:57:46.280 align:middle line:90%
Again, exploiting symmetry.

00:57:46.280 --> 00:57:48.170 align:middle line:90%
What do I gain?

00:57:48.170 --> 00:57:48.710 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:57:48.710 --> 00:57:51.800 align:middle line:84%
So I've got three
different gains.

00:57:51.800 --> 00:57:57.240 align:middle line:84%
First gain is, suppose that
a consumer visits me first.

00:57:57.240 --> 00:58:01.760 align:middle line:84%
That's half the
consumers visit me first.

00:58:01.760 --> 00:58:04.540 align:middle line:84%
When consumers visit
me first in x space--

00:58:04.540 --> 00:58:08.450 align:middle line:90%


00:58:08.450 --> 00:58:11.840 align:middle line:90%
in x space, it looks like this.

00:58:11.840 --> 00:58:14.300 align:middle line:90%
There's some x star.

00:58:14.300 --> 00:58:18.860 align:middle line:84%
And here they buy from me,
and here they search again.

00:58:18.860 --> 00:58:21.210 align:middle line:84%
If my match quality
is good, they buy.

00:58:21.210 --> 00:58:23.990 align:middle line:84%
If my match quality is
bad, they search again,

00:58:23.990 --> 00:58:27.530 align:middle line:90%
where x is 1 minus theta.

00:58:27.530 --> 00:58:28.830 align:middle line:90%
So match quality x.

00:58:28.830 --> 00:58:30.080 align:middle line:90%
High match quality is you buy.

00:58:30.080 --> 00:58:32.000 align:middle line:84%
Low match quality
is you search again.

00:58:32.000 --> 00:58:34.460 align:middle line:84%
So where I gain
from the price cut

00:58:34.460 --> 00:58:39.270 align:middle line:84%
is they visit me first
with probability x star.

00:58:39.270 --> 00:58:42.140 align:middle line:84%
They didn't like my
product all that much.

00:58:42.140 --> 00:58:45.470 align:middle line:90%
So they continue to search.

00:58:45.470 --> 00:58:48.020 align:middle line:84%
But then it turns
out that the amount

00:58:48.020 --> 00:58:50.210 align:middle line:84%
that they like the
other firm's product

00:58:50.210 --> 00:58:58.400 align:middle line:84%
is just ever so slightly
greater than x star and so close

00:58:58.400 --> 00:59:01.160 align:middle line:84%
to x star that my dp
price cut convinces them

00:59:01.160 --> 00:59:03.080 align:middle line:90%
to buy from me instead of him.

00:59:03.080 --> 00:59:07.890 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to happen with
probability dp divided by t.

00:59:07.890 --> 00:59:09.890 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to take
this coincidence of things

00:59:09.890 --> 00:59:12.968 align:middle line:84%
that they visit me,
they then visit him.

00:59:12.968 --> 00:59:14.760 align:middle line:84%
And then they like him
just about the same,

00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:17.540 align:middle line:84%
so my dp price cut
makes a difference.

00:59:17.540 --> 00:59:22.332 align:middle line:84%
There's also
consumers who visit--

00:59:22.332 --> 00:59:24.300 align:middle line:84%
oh, sorry, I did
that one backwards.

00:59:24.300 --> 00:59:27.230 align:middle line:84%
So there are also consumers
who visit the other firm first,

00:59:27.230 --> 00:59:29.030 align:middle line:84%
don't like the other
firm's product,

00:59:29.030 --> 00:59:31.530 align:middle line:84%
visit you, like
your product again,

00:59:31.530 --> 00:59:34.350 align:middle line:84%
almost exactly as much as they
like the other firm's product,

00:59:34.350 --> 00:59:36.840 align:middle line:84%
and then the dp price
cut makes a difference.

00:59:36.840 --> 00:59:42.980 align:middle line:84%
So we get these two terms that
are both 1/2 x star 1 over t dp.

00:59:42.980 --> 00:59:46.820 align:middle line:84%
The third effect is what we'll
call the search deterrence

00:59:46.820 --> 00:59:47.790 align:middle line:90%
effect.

00:59:47.790 --> 00:59:49.550 align:middle line:84%
The search deterrence
effect only

00:59:49.550 --> 00:59:53.270 align:middle line:84%
comes from consumers
who visit me first.

00:59:53.270 --> 00:59:58.020 align:middle line:84%
And they're consumers who visit
me first and would have bought

00:59:58.020 --> 01:00:01.350 align:middle line:84%
from firm 2 if they
went and visited firm 2

01:00:01.350 --> 01:00:06.180 align:middle line:84%
but who I convince not to
visit firm 2 with my price cut.

01:00:06.180 --> 01:00:10.290 align:middle line:84%
And so when I cut
my price by dp,

01:00:10.290 --> 01:00:14.740 align:middle line:84%
it's like I've made the match
slightly better by some amount.

01:00:14.740 --> 01:00:19.110 align:middle line:84%
It's like I increased the
match quality to x star plus dp

01:00:19.110 --> 01:00:21.930 align:middle line:90%
over t.

01:00:21.930 --> 01:00:24.780 align:middle line:84%
So I've made my match
slightly better.

01:00:24.780 --> 01:00:29.220 align:middle line:84%
I've shifted the-- oops, I've
made my price slightly better.

01:00:29.220 --> 01:00:37.560 align:middle line:84%
So by making my price
slightly better,

01:00:37.560 --> 01:00:40.240 align:middle line:90%
I'm shifting this region back.

01:00:40.240 --> 01:00:46.280 align:middle line:84%
So if the-- yeah,
if my price is--

01:00:46.280 --> 01:00:50.180 align:middle line:84%
if my price is lower, the
match quality in the--

01:00:50.180 --> 01:00:55.520 align:middle line:84%
quality space, they need to--
it's, like, x star shift back.

01:00:55.520 --> 01:00:57.295 align:middle line:84%
Because if my price
is better, they'll

01:00:57.295 --> 01:00:59.420 align:middle line:84%
take it even if it's a
slightly worse match quality

01:00:59.420 --> 01:01:01.250 align:middle line:90%
in characteristic space.

01:01:01.250 --> 01:01:07.350 align:middle line:84%
And so I shift that line to
the left by this distance.

01:01:07.350 --> 01:01:09.825 align:middle line:84%
And then if they would
have bought from me anyway,

01:01:09.825 --> 01:01:11.700 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't matter that
I deterred the search.

01:01:11.700 --> 01:01:14.060 align:middle line:84%
But if they would have
bought from the other guy,

01:01:14.060 --> 01:01:15.390 align:middle line:90%
then it is a gain.

01:01:15.390 --> 01:01:16.980 align:middle line:90%
And so I get these two effects.

01:01:16.980 --> 01:01:20.420 align:middle line:84%
One is the standard
Bertrand effect--

01:01:20.420 --> 01:01:23.333 align:middle line:84%
or the standard Hotelling
effect of the people

01:01:23.333 --> 01:01:25.250 align:middle line:84%
who see both prices and
they're close together

01:01:25.250 --> 01:01:26.370 align:middle line:90%
by the epsilon price cut.

01:01:26.370 --> 01:01:27.680 align:middle line:90%
I get those consumers.

01:01:27.680 --> 01:01:29.540 align:middle line:84%
And then I have
this extra effect

01:01:29.540 --> 01:01:32.400 align:middle line:90%
of search deterrence effect.

01:01:32.400 --> 01:01:34.220 align:middle line:84%
That is, by cutting my
price, I keep people

01:01:34.220 --> 01:01:35.940 align:middle line:90%
from searching the second firm.

01:01:35.940 --> 01:01:40.520 align:middle line:84%
And so it must be that
these three gains exactly

01:01:40.520 --> 01:01:43.537 align:middle line:90%
offset this one loss.

01:01:43.537 --> 01:01:45.870 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, and so what you do
is, the first-order condition

01:01:45.870 --> 01:01:48.600 align:middle line:84%
sets the gain equal to
the loss, you solve,

01:01:48.600 --> 01:01:51.720 align:middle line:90%
and it gives you that formula.

01:01:51.720 --> 01:01:54.570 align:middle line:90%
So that's the mechanics--

01:01:54.570 --> 01:01:56.590 align:middle line:84%
that's the mechanics of
finding an equilibrium.

01:01:56.590 --> 01:01:58.140 align:middle line:84%
And I guess the
key economic point

01:01:58.140 --> 01:02:01.800 align:middle line:84%
is just there are those two
tradeoffs-- the price comparison

01:02:01.800 --> 01:02:03.665 align:middle line:84%
effect and the search
deterrence effect.

01:02:03.665 --> 01:02:11.450 align:middle line:90%


01:02:11.450 --> 01:02:13.020 align:middle line:90%
Fixed Sample Search.

01:02:13.020 --> 01:02:14.522 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, any
questions on search

01:02:14.522 --> 01:02:15.730 align:middle line:90%
with differentiated products?

01:02:15.730 --> 01:02:18.500 align:middle line:90%


01:02:18.500 --> 01:02:23.110 align:middle line:84%
So fixed sample search,
in some applications,

01:02:23.110 --> 01:02:26.567 align:middle line:84%
the model we've been doing so
far has this idea of, you go out

01:02:26.567 --> 01:02:27.400 align:middle line:90%
and you do a search.

01:02:27.400 --> 01:02:29.567 align:middle line:84%
You see what you find,
whether you like the product.

01:02:29.567 --> 01:02:32.960 align:middle line:84%
You decide whether to stop here
and buy or go do another search.

01:02:32.960 --> 01:02:36.640 align:middle line:84%
There are some applications,
like getting contractors

01:02:36.640 --> 01:02:39.580 align:middle line:84%
to renovate your bathroom,
that you can't do

01:02:39.580 --> 01:02:41.050 align:middle line:90%
this sequential search process.

01:02:41.050 --> 01:02:45.355 align:middle line:84%
It takes a long time to get
a contractor to submit a bid.

01:02:45.355 --> 01:02:46.480 align:middle line:90%
You call them on the phone.

01:02:46.480 --> 01:02:48.100 align:middle line:84%
It takes the guy
forever to come.

01:02:48.100 --> 01:02:49.520 align:middle line:90%
He looks at your bathroom.

01:02:49.520 --> 01:02:50.900 align:middle line:90%
He says, I'll get back to you.

01:02:50.900 --> 01:02:52.462 align:middle line:90%
He doesn't get back to you.

01:02:52.462 --> 01:02:53.920 align:middle line:84%
A month later, he
finally works out

01:02:53.920 --> 01:02:55.490 align:middle line:90%
what he's going to charge you.

01:02:55.490 --> 01:02:58.240 align:middle line:84%
If you do that sequentially, you
know your bathroom will never

01:02:58.240 --> 01:02:59.260 align:middle line:90%
get renovated.

01:02:59.260 --> 01:03:02.590 align:middle line:84%
So what you do in practice--
and as governments--

01:03:02.590 --> 01:03:03.590 align:middle line:90%
we do this all the time.

01:03:03.590 --> 01:03:05.882 align:middle line:84%
We're renovating new space
in the economics department.

01:03:05.882 --> 01:03:08.810 align:middle line:84%
You try to get two or
three contractors to bid.

01:03:08.810 --> 01:03:12.180 align:middle line:84%
You say, OK, we're
getting three bids.

01:03:12.180 --> 01:03:14.053 align:middle line:84%
You may put a bid
deadline on them.

01:03:14.053 --> 01:03:16.220 align:middle line:84%
You may tell the guys, look,
I'm getting three bids.

01:03:16.220 --> 01:03:17.975 align:middle line:84%
I want all the bids
in by December 1.

01:03:17.975 --> 01:03:19.850 align:middle line:84%
If you don't get your
thing in by December 1,

01:03:19.850 --> 01:03:21.570 align:middle line:90%
I'm not going to consider you.

01:03:21.570 --> 01:03:24.380 align:middle line:84%
By December 1, you get three
bids and then you compare them

01:03:24.380 --> 01:03:25.470 align:middle line:90%
and then you're done.

01:03:25.470 --> 01:03:28.610 align:middle line:84%
But you're deciding
on how many bids

01:03:28.610 --> 01:03:31.527 align:middle line:84%
you're going to get before
you see what the bids are.

01:03:31.527 --> 01:03:33.110 align:middle line:84%
And that's the
difference between what

01:03:33.110 --> 01:03:36.330 align:middle line:84%
we call the fixed sample search
model versus a sequential model.

01:03:36.330 --> 01:03:38.900 align:middle line:84%
Sequential model, you
treat them one at a time

01:03:38.900 --> 01:03:40.460 align:middle line:90%
and decide whether to go on.

01:03:40.460 --> 01:03:46.030 align:middle line:84%
The fixed sample one, you
decide how many to get.

01:03:46.030 --> 01:03:50.950 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that these
models work somewhat similarly.

01:03:50.950 --> 01:03:53.440 align:middle line:84%
For a while, this
fixed sample model

01:03:53.440 --> 01:03:55.930 align:middle line:84%
was more popular in
structural empirical work

01:03:55.930 --> 01:04:00.616 align:middle line:84%
because the equations turn
out to be a little bit--

01:04:00.616 --> 01:04:02.800 align:middle line:84%
if you think about it,
in the sequential model,

01:04:02.800 --> 01:04:06.220 align:middle line:84%
the consumer is doing this
dynamic programming problem.

01:04:06.220 --> 01:04:07.960 align:middle line:84%
In the fixed sample
model, the consumer

01:04:07.960 --> 01:04:10.160 align:middle line:90%
is choosing a finite integer.

01:04:10.160 --> 01:04:12.100 align:middle line:84%
And so the choosing
a finite integer

01:04:12.100 --> 01:04:14.860 align:middle line:84%
is a much simpler strategy
space for the consumer

01:04:14.860 --> 01:04:17.775 align:middle line:84%
than this optimal dynamic
programming thing.

01:04:17.775 --> 01:04:19.150 align:middle line:84%
And so therefore,
there are a lot

01:04:19.150 --> 01:04:21.793 align:middle line:84%
of models that use the
fixed sample search.

01:04:21.793 --> 01:04:22.960 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes, it's appropriate.

01:04:22.960 --> 01:04:25.210 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, you think
sequential seems better.

01:04:25.210 --> 01:04:27.940 align:middle line:90%
But fixed does a lot of this.

01:04:27.940 --> 01:04:29.870 align:middle line:84%
It behaves in a lot
of ways similarly.

01:04:29.870 --> 01:04:31.828 align:middle line:84%
So let me go over how
you would solve that one.

01:04:31.828 --> 01:04:34.135 align:middle line:84%
So again, the game
is going to be--

01:04:34.135 --> 01:04:36.785 align:middle line:90%


01:04:36.785 --> 01:04:38.270 align:middle line:84%
oh, actually,
timing of the game.

01:04:38.270 --> 01:04:39.460 align:middle line:90%
I should have reversed this.

01:04:39.460 --> 01:04:41.920 align:middle line:84%
First stage, consumers
decide how many

01:04:41.920 --> 01:04:44.650 align:middle line:84%
searches k they're going--
quotes they're going to get.

01:04:44.650 --> 01:04:46.520 align:middle line:84%
Oh, no, actually,
timing the game first.

01:04:46.520 --> 01:04:50.690 align:middle line:84%
So first, the firms
simultaneously choose prices.

01:04:50.690 --> 01:04:52.210 align:middle line:90%
So I did the timing right.

01:04:52.210 --> 01:04:54.580 align:middle line:84%
The firms are choosing
prices, and they're

01:04:54.580 --> 01:04:56.120 align:middle line:90%
selling a homogeneous good--

01:04:56.120 --> 01:04:57.350 align:middle line:90%
renovating your bathroom.

01:04:57.350 --> 01:05:00.160 align:middle line:90%
Let's say it's homogeneous.

01:05:00.160 --> 01:05:01.970 align:middle line:84%
You have heterogeneous
consumers.

01:05:01.970 --> 01:05:04.390 align:middle line:84%
They have search costs s that
are distributed according

01:05:04.390 --> 01:05:08.950 align:middle line:84%
to G on some interval s lower
bar, s upper bar, like in Stahl.

01:05:08.950 --> 01:05:11.560 align:middle line:90%
No shoppers here.

01:05:11.560 --> 01:05:13.510 align:middle line:84%
And then the consumers
are going to,

01:05:13.510 --> 01:05:16.000 align:middle line:90%
again, have multiunit demands.

01:05:16.000 --> 01:05:19.000 align:middle line:84%
And what the consumers
do is they choose

01:05:19.000 --> 01:05:20.810 align:middle line:90%
how many price quotes to get.

01:05:20.810 --> 01:05:24.490 align:middle line:84%
So the timing of the game is,
the firms choose their prices.

01:05:24.490 --> 01:05:27.740 align:middle line:84%
Each consumer decides how
many quotes they want.

01:05:27.740 --> 01:05:31.090 align:middle line:84%
They then get the quotes
by sampling randomly

01:05:31.090 --> 01:05:31.960 align:middle line:90%
from the firms--

01:05:31.960 --> 01:05:33.730 align:middle line:90%
set of firms.

01:05:33.730 --> 01:05:36.130 align:middle line:84%
And then the consumers
buy from whichever

01:05:36.130 --> 01:05:37.425 align:middle line:90%
firm gave them the best price.

01:05:37.425 --> 01:05:42.530 align:middle line:90%


01:05:42.530 --> 01:05:46.560 align:middle line:84%
So again, as in that
Stahl model I did,

01:05:46.560 --> 01:05:49.260 align:middle line:84%
this is going to be a model
that has a mixed strategy

01:05:49.260 --> 01:05:50.610 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

01:05:50.610 --> 01:05:53.825 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to have a
mixed strategy equilibrium--

01:05:53.825 --> 01:05:55.507 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't look
exactly like these,

01:05:55.507 --> 01:05:57.340 align:middle line:84%
but it's going to be
the same kind of thing.

01:05:57.340 --> 01:06:01.410 align:middle line:84%
There's going to be some
density f of p on some interval

01:06:01.410 --> 01:06:02.810 align:middle line:90%
p lower bar, p upper bar.

01:06:02.810 --> 01:06:07.190 align:middle line:90%


01:06:07.190 --> 01:06:10.730 align:middle line:84%
If that's what happens, firms
mix over some interval p

01:06:10.730 --> 01:06:13.100 align:middle line:84%
lower bar, p upper
bar, what's your value

01:06:13.100 --> 01:06:17.030 align:middle line:90%
from getting k price quotes?

01:06:17.030 --> 01:06:19.310 align:middle line:84%
Your value from
getting k price quotes

01:06:19.310 --> 01:06:21.950 align:middle line:84%
is you're just going to
integrate over from p lower bar

01:06:21.950 --> 01:06:27.830 align:middle line:84%
to p upper bar, consumer
surplus of x times f of 1 colon

01:06:27.830 --> 01:06:30.680 align:middle line:84%
k of x dx, where f of
1 colon k I'm defining

01:06:30.680 --> 01:06:33.110 align:middle line:84%
to be the first-order
statistic of k

01:06:33.110 --> 01:06:34.860 align:middle line:90%
draws from that distribution.

01:06:34.860 --> 01:06:38.060 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm getting k draws
from that distribution f,

01:06:38.060 --> 01:06:42.680 align:middle line:84%
what is the density of
the lowest of the k draws?

01:06:42.680 --> 01:06:45.860 align:middle line:84%
And the density of the lowest of
k draws from that distribution

01:06:45.860 --> 01:06:47.900 align:middle line:90%
is--

01:06:47.900 --> 01:06:49.370 align:middle line:90%
what's this?

01:06:49.370 --> 01:06:51.650 align:middle line:84%
Probability that the
lowest draw is exactly

01:06:51.650 --> 01:06:57.090 align:middle line:84%
x is k times f of x times
1 minus f to the k minus 1.

01:06:57.090 --> 01:07:00.620 align:middle line:84%
So there's one firm
offering price exactly x,

01:07:00.620 --> 01:07:04.520 align:middle line:84%
which happens with probability k
f of x, and then all other firms

01:07:04.520 --> 01:07:05.570 align:middle line:90%
have higher prices.

01:07:05.570 --> 01:07:08.280 align:middle line:90%


01:07:08.280 --> 01:07:10.310 align:middle line:84%
So if this is the
distribution, and then I

01:07:10.310 --> 01:07:14.030 align:middle line:84%
say, what's the distribution
of the best of two

01:07:14.030 --> 01:07:16.940 align:middle line:84%
draws in that distribution, it's
got more mass here and less mass

01:07:16.940 --> 01:07:19.350 align:middle line:84%
here because I'm more likely
to find something big.

01:07:19.350 --> 01:07:21.200 align:middle line:84%
If I do a hundred
draws, I'm very

01:07:21.200 --> 01:07:24.410 align:middle line:84%
likely to find the lowest
price and very unlikely to find

01:07:24.410 --> 01:07:25.160 align:middle line:90%
the highest price.

01:07:25.160 --> 01:07:27.800 align:middle line:84%
This is the best in my
sample of a hundred.

01:07:27.800 --> 01:07:29.300 align:middle line:90%
So anyway, I'm choosing k.

01:07:29.300 --> 01:07:31.230 align:middle line:84%
That affects the density
of the lowest price.

01:07:31.230 --> 01:07:33.620 align:middle line:84%
And then this becomes
my utility that I'm

01:07:33.620 --> 01:07:35.480 align:middle line:84%
going to get from
buying from that firm,

01:07:35.480 --> 01:07:36.955 align:middle line:90%
I guess, minus the search cost.

01:07:36.955 --> 01:07:39.740 align:middle line:90%


01:07:39.740 --> 01:07:43.010 align:middle line:84%
Notice that this model
has a natural decreasing

01:07:43.010 --> 01:07:45.740 align:middle line:84%
marginal returns
to search property.

01:07:45.740 --> 01:07:48.440 align:middle line:84%
The value of the
first search is bigger

01:07:48.440 --> 01:07:50.697 align:middle line:84%
than the incremental
value of the second search

01:07:50.697 --> 01:07:53.030 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than the incremental
value of the third search

01:07:53.030 --> 01:07:54.590 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

01:07:54.590 --> 01:07:55.770 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

01:07:55.770 --> 01:07:58.100 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's because
the incremental value

01:07:58.100 --> 01:08:02.180 align:middle line:84%
of the k-th search is just I'm
integrating over all prices

01:08:02.180 --> 01:08:04.340 align:middle line:84%
that I might find
on the k-th search

01:08:04.340 --> 01:08:08.768 align:middle line:84%
v of x times the density
of the best price.

01:08:08.768 --> 01:08:10.310 align:middle line:84%
So think about, I'm
just conditioning

01:08:10.310 --> 01:08:13.650 align:middle line:84%
on the best price I've found in
the first k minus 1 searches.

01:08:13.650 --> 01:08:16.399 align:middle line:84%
And so the best price I found
in the first k minus 1 searches

01:08:16.399 --> 01:08:17.490 align:middle line:90%
was x.

01:08:17.490 --> 01:08:20.540 align:middle line:84%
I'm now going to get v of x from
an extra search, where v of x

01:08:20.540 --> 01:08:24.550 align:middle line:84%
is that quantity I gave
you before, that integral.

01:08:24.550 --> 01:08:29.479 align:middle line:84%
And so as v is an
increasing function,

01:08:29.479 --> 01:08:31.520 align:middle line:84%
the higher the best
price I've found,

01:08:31.520 --> 01:08:33.740 align:middle line:90%
the higher is my gain to search.

01:08:33.740 --> 01:08:37.939 align:middle line:84%
But then as k increases,
these distributions

01:08:37.939 --> 01:08:42.510 align:middle line:84%
are decreasing in a first-order
stochastic dominance sense.

01:08:42.510 --> 01:08:44.720 align:middle line:84%
And so because I've
already found a good price,

01:08:44.720 --> 01:08:47.569 align:middle line:84%
the gain from a new
search is reduced.

01:08:47.569 --> 01:08:51.590 align:middle line:90%
So once I've already found--

01:08:51.590 --> 01:08:53.460 align:middle line:84%
if I've already done
a thousand searches,

01:08:53.460 --> 01:08:55.460 align:middle line:84%
I've probably already
found something very close

01:08:55.460 --> 01:08:56.418 align:middle line:90%
to the lowest possible.

01:08:56.418 --> 01:08:58.279 align:middle line:84%
So the value of one
extra search is small.

01:08:58.279 --> 01:09:01.790 align:middle line:84%
And it just has that-- it's
got that monotonicity property.

01:09:01.790 --> 01:09:03.606 align:middle line:84%
Every extra search,
it's more likely

01:09:03.606 --> 01:09:05.189 align:middle line:84%
that the best price
I've found is low.

01:09:05.189 --> 01:09:07.481 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, it's more likely
my gain from search is low.

01:09:07.481 --> 01:09:11.040 align:middle line:90%


01:09:11.040 --> 01:09:13.649 align:middle line:84%
So what do we get
in equilibrium?

01:09:13.649 --> 01:09:15.270 align:middle line:84%
In equilibrium, we
get a graph that

01:09:15.270 --> 01:09:21.180 align:middle line:84%
looks like this, where, if
your search cost is less than--

01:09:21.180 --> 01:09:27.569 align:middle line:84%
this line here would be at
U of N minus U of N minus 1,

01:09:27.569 --> 01:09:31.260 align:middle line:84%
or U sub N minus
U sub N minus 1--

01:09:31.260 --> 01:09:35.040 align:middle line:84%
if your search cost is below
that, you'll visit every firm.

01:09:35.040 --> 01:09:38.010 align:middle line:84%
If your search cost is
above that but less than U

01:09:38.010 --> 01:09:42.790 align:middle line:84%
of N minus 1 minus U of N minus
2, you'll visit N minus 1 firms.

01:09:42.790 --> 01:09:46.090 align:middle line:84%
If your search cost is higher,
you'll visit N minus 2 firms,

01:09:46.090 --> 01:09:46.590 align:middle line:90%
so on.

01:09:46.590 --> 01:09:47.189 align:middle line:90%
You go all the way out.

01:09:47.189 --> 01:09:49.290 align:middle line:84%
There's some consumers
who visit one firm,

01:09:49.290 --> 01:09:51.915 align:middle line:84%
and there may be some consumers
with s is high enough that they

01:09:51.915 --> 01:09:53.533 align:middle line:90%
would visit zero firms.

01:09:53.533 --> 01:09:55.200 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going
to get is the search

01:09:55.200 --> 01:09:58.647 align:middle line:84%
costs are just going to
divide up in these intervals.

01:09:58.647 --> 01:10:00.480 align:middle line:84%
So there's going to be
an interval of people

01:10:00.480 --> 01:10:04.080 align:middle line:84%
who do N, N minus 1,
N minus 2, and so on.

01:10:04.080 --> 01:10:09.270 align:middle line:84%
And then remember, I had some
density capital G on the search

01:10:09.270 --> 01:10:11.070 align:middle line:90%
costs on some interval.

01:10:11.070 --> 01:10:15.480 align:middle line:84%
So then integrating that density
over these regions is going

01:10:15.480 --> 01:10:19.805 align:middle line:90%
to give you the-- so if I--

01:10:19.805 --> 01:10:21.180 align:middle line:84%
don't want to
write on my screen.

01:10:21.180 --> 01:10:26.650 align:middle line:90%


01:10:26.650 --> 01:10:29.920 align:middle line:84%
I have these regions
where you do N searches,

01:10:29.920 --> 01:10:35.170 align:middle line:84%
N minus 1 searches,
N minus 2 searches,

01:10:35.170 --> 01:10:38.050 align:middle line:84%
all the way out to this region
where you do one search.

01:10:38.050 --> 01:10:40.630 align:middle line:84%
And then there's some
density of search costs

01:10:40.630 --> 01:10:42.200 align:middle line:90%
that looks like that.

01:10:42.200 --> 01:10:45.250 align:middle line:90%
This is the g of s.

01:10:45.250 --> 01:10:47.590 align:middle line:84%
The integral of g
over this region

01:10:47.590 --> 01:10:52.130 align:middle line:84%
is the mass qN of people
who do exactly N searches.

01:10:52.130 --> 01:10:58.120 align:middle line:84%
And the integral over this
region, this is qN minus 1,

01:10:58.120 --> 01:11:00.617 align:middle line:84%
the mass of people who
do N minus 1 searches.

01:11:00.617 --> 01:11:02.200 align:middle line:84%
And then I go all
the way out to here.

01:11:02.200 --> 01:11:03.492 align:middle line:90%
The integral over this region--

01:11:03.492 --> 01:11:06.170 align:middle line:90%


01:11:06.170 --> 01:11:09.710 align:middle line:84%
these are the people
who do one search.

01:11:09.710 --> 01:11:11.810 align:middle line:84%
So just integrating
the density g

01:11:11.810 --> 01:11:15.110 align:middle line:84%
over these boundary regions
of how many searches you do

01:11:15.110 --> 01:11:17.990 align:middle line:84%
gives me the number
of people who do

01:11:17.990 --> 01:11:19.490 align:middle line:84%
one search, the
number of people who

01:11:19.490 --> 01:11:21.642 align:middle line:84%
do two searches,
the number of people

01:11:21.642 --> 01:11:23.600 align:middle line:84%
who do N minus 1 searches,
the number of people

01:11:23.600 --> 01:11:24.490 align:middle line:90%
who do N searches.

01:11:24.490 --> 01:11:29.270 align:middle line:90%


01:11:29.270 --> 01:11:33.860 align:middle line:84%
And then just like in
Stahl, we're going to get--

01:11:33.860 --> 01:11:37.145 align:middle line:84%
the fact that-- if you think
about Stahl, Stahl was--

01:11:37.145 --> 01:11:42.200 align:middle line:84%
the solvable equation of Stahl
I did for you was q1 was mu,

01:11:42.200 --> 01:11:48.680 align:middle line:84%
qN was 1 minus mu, all
these other were 0.

01:11:48.680 --> 01:11:51.290 align:middle line:84%
That's what I did for you, is
there are a bunch of people

01:11:51.290 --> 01:11:54.590 align:middle line:84%
doing one search, a bunch
of people doing N searches,

01:11:54.590 --> 01:11:58.820 align:middle line:84%
and then nobody else, in that
one case I solved for you.

01:11:58.820 --> 01:12:02.900 align:middle line:84%
In this model, all these q's
are going to be non-zero,

01:12:02.900 --> 01:12:06.640 align:middle line:84%
but you're going to solve this
model in exactly the same way.

01:12:06.640 --> 01:12:10.443 align:middle line:84%
You're going to say, I know
what the distribution of--

01:12:10.443 --> 01:12:11.610 align:middle line:90%
I know what the profits are.

01:12:11.610 --> 01:12:13.490 align:middle line:84%
In this case, the
profits that I know

01:12:13.490 --> 01:12:19.640 align:middle line:84%
is I know that the profits, if
I charge the monopoly price,

01:12:19.640 --> 01:12:30.790 align:middle line:84%
are going to be pm minus c D
of pm times q1 times 1 over N.

01:12:30.790 --> 01:12:35.445 align:middle line:84%
If I charge-- the p upper bar
is going to be pm in this model,

01:12:35.445 --> 01:12:35.945 align:middle line:90%
always.

01:12:35.945 --> 01:12:39.360 align:middle line:90%


01:12:39.360 --> 01:12:44.160 align:middle line:84%
So if I charge the highest
possible price, my profit is--

01:12:44.160 --> 01:12:45.750 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to sell
to the consumer

01:12:45.750 --> 01:12:48.070 align:middle line:84%
if and only if
they visit me first

01:12:48.070 --> 01:12:50.370 align:middle line:84%
and they're one of the
consumers who decided

01:12:50.370 --> 01:12:52.180 align:middle line:90%
to only get one price quote.

01:12:52.180 --> 01:12:55.420 align:middle line:84%
So the high s people here decide
to get exactly one price quote.

01:12:55.420 --> 01:12:59.010 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to sell to them if
they get exactly one price quote

01:12:59.010 --> 01:13:01.140 align:middle line:90%
and that one price quote is me.

01:13:01.140 --> 01:13:03.630 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to rip them
off with a monopoly price.

01:13:03.630 --> 01:13:09.180 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to be my
profit, pm minus c D of pm.

01:13:09.180 --> 01:13:13.860 align:middle line:84%
What do I get if I
charge any other price?

01:13:13.860 --> 01:13:21.210 align:middle line:84%
Well, I'm going to get, again,
p minus c D of pm per consumer.

01:13:21.210 --> 01:13:24.540 align:middle line:84%
But then how many consumers
am I going to get?

01:13:24.540 --> 01:13:28.740 align:middle line:84%
Well, if a consumer
visits me first--

01:13:28.740 --> 01:13:31.590 align:middle line:84%
if a consumer is doing one
search and visits me first,

01:13:31.590 --> 01:13:33.540 align:middle line:90%
they always buy.

01:13:33.540 --> 01:13:39.450 align:middle line:84%
But then also, there are some
consumers who visit two firms.

01:13:39.450 --> 01:13:43.290 align:middle line:84%
They visit me with
probability 2 over N,

01:13:43.290 --> 01:13:46.170 align:middle line:84%
and then I sell to
them with probability 1

01:13:46.170 --> 01:13:48.325 align:middle line:90%
minus capital F of p.

01:13:48.325 --> 01:13:50.700 align:middle line:84%
Because it has to be they
visit two firms-- they visit me

01:13:50.700 --> 01:13:53.940 align:middle line:84%
and somebody else charging
an even higher price.

01:13:53.940 --> 01:13:58.050 align:middle line:84%
And then there are also
consumers who visit three firms.

01:13:58.050 --> 01:14:01.260 align:middle line:84%
They visit me with
probability 3 over N.

01:14:01.260 --> 01:14:03.180 align:middle line:84%
And they buy from me
with probability 1

01:14:03.180 --> 01:14:05.550 align:middle line:90%
minus f of p squared.

01:14:05.550 --> 01:14:08.310 align:middle line:84%
Because it has to be, they
visit three firms, I'm included.

01:14:08.310 --> 01:14:11.940 align:middle line:84%
And the other two firms turn out
to have higher prices than me.

01:14:11.940 --> 01:14:17.520 align:middle line:84%
Well, again, this becomes
an n-th degree polynomial

01:14:17.520 --> 01:14:20.410 align:middle line:84%
instead of something that just
has 1 minus f in one place.

01:14:20.410 --> 01:14:21.930 align:middle line:90%
But you can still--

01:14:21.930 --> 01:14:24.360 align:middle line:84%
this equals this
lets you solve for f

01:14:24.360 --> 01:14:27.690 align:middle line:90%
of p for every p less than pm.

01:14:27.690 --> 01:14:30.510 align:middle line:84%
And so it's the same kind
of argument as in Stahl.

01:14:30.510 --> 01:14:33.690 align:middle line:84%
It's like, if you
knew q1 through qN,

01:14:33.690 --> 01:14:38.010 align:middle line:84%
you could solve for what
capital F would have to be.

01:14:38.010 --> 01:14:40.660 align:middle line:84%
But then in this model, there
is a fixed-point calculation.

01:14:40.660 --> 01:14:44.700 align:middle line:84%
You say, OK, here's my guess
for what q1 through qN are.

01:14:44.700 --> 01:14:46.860 align:middle line:90%
I guess q1 through qN.

01:14:46.860 --> 01:14:50.340 align:middle line:84%
I figure out what capital
F would have to be.

01:14:50.340 --> 01:14:53.140 align:middle line:84%
Once I figured out what
capital F would be,

01:14:53.140 --> 01:14:55.830 align:middle line:84%
I do this computation
of what the U's

01:14:55.830 --> 01:14:58.900 align:middle line:84%
are to figure out how many
consumers search N things.

01:14:58.900 --> 01:15:01.830 align:middle line:84%
And I see if I reproduce
the q1 through qN.

01:15:01.830 --> 01:15:04.170 align:middle line:84%
And so numerically, I
solve this by finding

01:15:04.170 --> 01:15:05.610 align:middle line:90%
a fixed-point problem.

01:15:05.610 --> 01:15:07.680 align:middle line:90%
I guess q1 through qN.

01:15:07.680 --> 01:15:09.460 align:middle line:84%
I figure out what
f would have to be.

01:15:09.460 --> 01:15:10.930 align:middle line:84%
I figure out what
U would then be.

01:15:10.930 --> 01:15:12.390 align:middle line:84%
I figure out what the
q's would then be,

01:15:12.390 --> 01:15:13.390 align:middle line:90%
and I see if they match.

01:15:13.390 --> 01:15:15.900 align:middle line:84%
If not, I have an
optimizer moving

01:15:15.900 --> 01:15:19.380 align:middle line:84%
the q's around to try to get the
q's to match the q's I started

01:15:19.380 --> 01:15:20.825 align:middle line:90%
with until I find a fixed point.

01:15:20.825 --> 01:15:23.850 align:middle line:90%


01:15:23.850 --> 01:15:25.650 align:middle line:84%
Again, it's not
something theorists

01:15:25.650 --> 01:15:28.330 align:middle line:84%
do often because it doesn't
have a nice closed form.

01:15:28.330 --> 01:15:31.410 align:middle line:84%
But it is something that
empirical people will often

01:15:31.410 --> 01:15:34.270 align:middle line:84%
do if they're solving their
model numerically anyway.

01:15:34.270 --> 01:15:36.900 align:middle line:84%
It's easier than the
dynamic programming thing

01:15:36.900 --> 01:15:38.800 align:middle line:90%
that the other one has in it.

01:15:38.800 --> 01:15:42.250 align:middle line:90%


01:15:42.250 --> 01:15:43.486 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

01:15:43.486 --> 01:15:48.820 align:middle line:90%


01:15:48.820 --> 01:15:51.597 align:middle line:84%
I have just two brief slides
I'll do. and then the other one,

01:15:51.597 --> 01:15:52.180 align:middle line:90%
I'm actually--

01:15:52.180 --> 01:15:54.138 align:middle line:84%
I think I'm going to let
[? Rowey ?] talk about

01:15:54.138 --> 01:15:55.480 align:middle line:90%
on Friday.

01:15:55.480 --> 01:15:57.920 align:middle line:90%
So obfuscation.

01:15:57.920 --> 01:16:04.240 align:middle line:84%
So an observation about all
these search cost models is--

01:16:04.240 --> 01:16:07.810 align:middle line:84%
so far today, I've always taken
the s as an exogenous parameter.

01:16:07.810 --> 01:16:11.230 align:middle line:84%
And then profits of the
firms have gone up in s.

01:16:11.230 --> 01:16:15.490 align:middle line:84%
So that raised the question
of, where do s's come from?

01:16:15.490 --> 01:16:19.030 align:middle line:84%
Why are there search
costs in models?

01:16:19.030 --> 01:16:23.900 align:middle line:84%
And clearly, firms collectively
are better off when s is bigger.

01:16:23.900 --> 01:16:27.333 align:middle line:84%
The bigger is s, the higher
are the firm profits.

01:16:27.333 --> 01:16:28.750 align:middle line:84%
And so certainly,
if all the firms

01:16:28.750 --> 01:16:30.400 align:middle line:84%
could get together
and agree, are

01:16:30.400 --> 01:16:32.390 align:middle line:84%
we going to make searching for
a mattress easier or are we

01:16:32.390 --> 01:16:34.550 align:middle line:84%
going to make searching for
a price on a mattress hard,

01:16:34.550 --> 01:16:35.530 align:middle line:84%
all the firms will
get together and say,

01:16:35.530 --> 01:16:37.613 align:middle line:84%
let's make searching really
hard for the consumers

01:16:37.613 --> 01:16:42.820 align:middle line:84%
and we can all charge a
monopoly price and be best off.

01:16:42.820 --> 01:16:44.590 align:middle line:84%
But that raises the
question of, does that

01:16:44.590 --> 01:16:48.120 align:middle line:84%
require collusion among
the firms to get s high?

01:16:48.120 --> 01:16:51.320 align:middle line:84%
Or can firms also
individually have an incentive

01:16:51.320 --> 01:16:56.400 align:middle line:84%
to raise their s, even if
it's just individually,

01:16:56.400 --> 01:16:57.767 align:middle line:90%
do they want to raise s?

01:16:57.767 --> 01:16:59.600 align:middle line:84%
And then you might
think, well, that's going

01:16:59.600 --> 01:17:00.767 align:middle line:90%
to be harder to get to work.

01:17:00.767 --> 01:17:02.360 align:middle line:84%
Because if I raise
my s and consumers

01:17:02.360 --> 01:17:04.730 align:middle line:84%
see that I'm the
annoying firm to visit,

01:17:04.730 --> 01:17:06.920 align:middle line:84%
aren't they all going to
go to the nonannoying firm

01:17:06.920 --> 01:17:09.540 align:middle line:84%
first and pay less
to get s there?

01:17:09.540 --> 01:17:11.870 align:middle line:84%
And isn't that bad for me
if I raise my search cost

01:17:11.870 --> 01:17:14.210 align:middle line:84%
and I drive consumers
to the other firms?

01:17:14.210 --> 01:17:18.890 align:middle line:84%
And there are a few papers that
discuss reasons why you actually

01:17:18.890 --> 01:17:21.650 align:middle line:84%
can get it to be individually
rational for firms

01:17:21.650 --> 01:17:25.550 align:middle line:84%
to raise search costs and not
just collectively rational.

01:17:25.550 --> 01:17:32.720 align:middle line:84%
So one paper I put is this paper
by Chris Wilson in the IJIO.

01:17:32.720 --> 01:17:36.410 align:middle line:84%
And this considers this game,
which has a surprising result.

01:17:36.410 --> 01:17:40.480 align:middle line:84%
Firms simultaneously choose
search costs, s1, s2, from s

01:17:40.480 --> 01:17:42.350 align:middle line:90%
lower bar, s upper bar.

01:17:42.350 --> 01:17:45.680 align:middle line:84%
The consumers and the firms
observe what these search costs

01:17:45.680 --> 01:17:49.550 align:middle line:84%
are, and then the firms
choose an unobserved price,

01:17:49.550 --> 01:17:54.170 align:middle line:84%
and then consumers follow a
search and purchase policy

01:17:54.170 --> 01:17:56.810 align:middle line:90%
with optimal sequential search.

01:17:56.810 --> 01:17:59.900 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that
this model does not

01:17:59.900 --> 01:18:03.590 align:middle line:84%
have an equilibrium where both
firms set price s lower bar.

01:18:03.590 --> 01:18:06.020 align:middle line:84%
So it is true that you
try to have a firm set

01:18:06.020 --> 01:18:07.760 align:middle line:90%
price s lower bar.

01:18:07.760 --> 01:18:10.250 align:middle line:84%
You have this one
firm, what it can

01:18:10.250 --> 01:18:12.530 align:middle line:90%
do is it can raise its price.

01:18:12.530 --> 01:18:15.703 align:middle line:84%
It can raise its price and be
known as the annoying firm,

01:18:15.703 --> 01:18:18.120 align:middle line:84%
so that all the consumers are
going to go to firm 1 first.

01:18:18.120 --> 01:18:21.740 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm firm 2, I
raise my search cost,

01:18:21.740 --> 01:18:25.190 align:middle line:84%
and then I get everyone to
visit the other firm first.

01:18:25.190 --> 01:18:28.800 align:middle line:84%
But then the other firm knows
that it's getting visited first.

01:18:28.800 --> 01:18:31.250 align:middle line:84%
And so because it knows that
it's getting visited first,

01:18:31.250 --> 01:18:32.210 align:middle line:84%
consumers are going
to pay a search

01:18:32.210 --> 01:18:33.720 align:middle line:90%
cost to go to the second firm.

01:18:33.720 --> 01:18:34.700 align:middle line:84%
It's going to have
a big incentive

01:18:34.700 --> 01:18:36.200 align:middle line:84%
to raise its price
because it knows,

01:18:36.200 --> 01:18:38.390 align:middle line:84%
I can at least go s
bigger than the other guy,

01:18:38.390 --> 01:18:40.640 align:middle line:84%
and people are going
to not go visit him.

01:18:40.640 --> 01:18:44.270 align:middle line:84%
But then, of
course, once it goes

01:18:44.270 --> 01:18:46.743 align:middle line:84%
s bigger than the
other guy, it's

01:18:46.743 --> 01:18:48.660 align:middle line:84%
going to have an incentive
to raise its price.

01:18:48.660 --> 01:18:50.330 align:middle line:84%
Then firm 2 is also
going to say, well,

01:18:50.330 --> 01:18:52.872 align:middle line:84%
I'm only getting the people who
visited firm 1 and then found

01:18:52.872 --> 01:18:55.072 align:middle line:84%
out that firm 1's price
was unexpectedly high.

01:18:55.072 --> 01:18:57.030 align:middle line:84%
And so then they're going
to raise their price.

01:18:57.030 --> 01:19:02.780 align:middle line:84%
And you can create this
asymmetric Bertrand-like

01:19:02.780 --> 01:19:05.240 align:middle line:84%
competition, where the
firms have an incentive

01:19:05.240 --> 01:19:06.170 align:middle line:90%
to raise their price.

01:19:06.170 --> 01:19:07.587 align:middle line:84%
And then because
I know my rival's

01:19:07.587 --> 01:19:10.020 align:middle line:84%
going to raise their price,
I may raise my price too.

01:19:10.020 --> 01:19:12.800 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, even though
they're starting with him,

01:19:12.800 --> 01:19:14.840 align:middle line:84%
he chooses a distribution--
a mixed strategy

01:19:14.840 --> 01:19:17.240 align:middle line:84%
that's so high that
people come to me anyway,

01:19:17.240 --> 01:19:18.890 align:middle line:90%
and we're both better off.

01:19:18.890 --> 01:19:22.310 align:middle line:90%
So that kind of story can work.

01:19:22.310 --> 01:19:26.390 align:middle line:84%
The other story that I have
in a paper with Alex Wolitzky

01:19:26.390 --> 01:19:31.100 align:middle line:84%
is that, you can
also do this when

01:19:31.100 --> 01:19:32.720 align:middle line:84%
the cost of getting
a price quote

01:19:32.720 --> 01:19:34.830 align:middle line:90%
is not observable in advance.

01:19:34.830 --> 01:19:37.238 align:middle line:84%
Suppose that it's only when
you get to the car dealer

01:19:37.238 --> 01:19:39.530 align:middle line:84%
that you learn whether it
takes one hour to get a price

01:19:39.530 --> 01:19:41.510 align:middle line:84%
quote, or five hours to get a
price quote from that dealer,

01:19:41.510 --> 01:19:43.385 align:middle line:84%
and how long they're
going to make you haggle

01:19:43.385 --> 01:19:45.870 align:middle line:84%
before they get the real price
they're willing to offer.

01:19:45.870 --> 01:19:49.650 align:middle line:84%
And we just discuss in our
paper other ways you can do it.

01:19:49.650 --> 01:19:53.150 align:middle line:84%
One way you can do this is with
the convex disutility of time

01:19:53.150 --> 01:19:55.490 align:middle line:84%
spent shopping,
where after you've

01:19:55.490 --> 01:19:57.440 align:middle line:84%
spent 5 hours in a car
dealer, you now really

01:19:57.440 --> 01:20:00.065 align:middle line:84%
don't want to spend 10 hours by
going to the second car dealer.

01:20:00.065 --> 01:20:02.240 align:middle line:90%
And there's a convex time cost.

01:20:02.240 --> 01:20:05.570 align:middle line:84%
The other is if you add
uncertainty over how efficient

01:20:05.570 --> 01:20:07.220 align:middle line:90%
it could be to buy a car.

01:20:07.220 --> 01:20:10.760 align:middle line:84%
And this one I think works
well with getting a quote

01:20:10.760 --> 01:20:12.680 align:middle line:90%
from a contractor.

01:20:12.680 --> 01:20:14.783 align:middle line:84%
You get one contractor,
and you spent 14 hours

01:20:14.783 --> 01:20:16.950 align:middle line:84%
with this contractor trying
to get a quote from him.

01:20:16.950 --> 01:20:18.440 align:middle line:84%
You think getting a
quote from another guy

01:20:18.440 --> 01:20:20.420 align:middle line:84%
is also going to take
you 14 hours because it

01:20:20.420 --> 01:20:21.690 align:middle line:90%
must be that hard.

01:20:21.690 --> 01:20:24.470 align:middle line:84%
And if consumers don't know how
hard getting price quotes are,

01:20:24.470 --> 01:20:26.870 align:middle line:84%
by making it more
annoying, people

01:20:26.870 --> 01:20:30.080 align:middle line:84%
are going to infer that
the other dealers are also

01:20:30.080 --> 01:20:31.110 align:middle line:90%
more annoying.

01:20:31.110 --> 01:20:33.770 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, that's going to
make you also individually raise

01:20:33.770 --> 01:20:34.970 align:middle line:90%
search costs.

01:20:34.970 --> 01:20:36.830 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so that's
the argument there

01:20:36.830 --> 01:20:40.880 align:middle line:84%
for there are reasons why, even
though you lose some customers,

01:20:40.880 --> 01:20:43.370 align:middle line:84%
you may want to
raise search costs.

01:20:43.370 --> 01:20:45.870 align:middle line:84%
So let me say, plan for
the rest of the week.

01:20:45.870 --> 01:20:48.210 align:middle line:84%
I think I'm going to
have [? Rowey ?] talk

01:20:48.210 --> 01:20:50.055 align:middle line:90%
about multiproduct search.

01:20:50.055 --> 01:20:52.180 align:middle line:84%
We don't have any problem
set to go over on Friday.

01:20:52.180 --> 01:20:54.730 align:middle line:84%
So I think he's going to talk
about a couple recent papers.

01:20:54.730 --> 01:20:56.730 align:middle line:84%
This paper on
multiproduct search

01:20:56.730 --> 01:20:58.440 align:middle line:90%
is likely to be one of them.

01:20:58.440 --> 01:21:03.000 align:middle line:84%
And then next week, I'm going to
jump ahead to empirical search.

01:21:03.000 --> 01:21:05.547 align:middle line:84%
And these are the three
papers I plan to talk about.

01:21:05.547 --> 01:21:07.380 align:middle line:84%
There's no class Monday
due to the holidays,

01:21:07.380 --> 01:21:09.597 align:middle line:90%
so it's next Wednesday.

01:21:09.597 --> 01:21:11.430 align:middle line:84%
And then if you want
to read one in advance,

01:21:11.430 --> 01:21:17.076 align:middle line:84%
I would read my paper with
Sara on search and obfuscation.

01:21:17.076 --> 01:21:24.000 align:middle line:90%